Hey everybody, welcome to the podcast.

2
00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:04.020
My guest today is Joe De Sena.

3
00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:06.620
Joe is the founder of Spartan,

4
00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:08.630
the whole Spartan Race Series.

5
00:00:08.630 --> 00:00:10.900
This is an incredible conversation.

6
00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:15.760
I think it's designed to really inspire how you think about

7
00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:19.470
the new year and get you off on the right trajectory

8
00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:21.300
as we think about 2021.

9
00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:24.090
So without further ado, hit that subscribe button

10
00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:28.257
and please enjoy my conversation with Joe De Sena.

11
00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:36.840
Right on man, so good to see you.

12
00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:39.390
We've been trying to make this happen for so long.

13
00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:40.925 line:15%
It's taken forever, man.

14
00:00:40.925 --> 00:00:42.535 line:15%
I don't know how many, gotta be three or four years.

15
00:00:42.535 --> 00:00:44.410 line:15%
Couple of years, I think the last time I saw you,

16
00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:46.690 line:15%
I did your show when you guys

17
00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:48.960
were hosting an event at Dodgers Stadium.

18
00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:50.380
Yeah, I think like a last minute,

19
00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:53.094
hey Rich, can you come over to the stadium?

20
00:00:53.094 --> 00:00:55.070
And we caught you and we did some filming

21
00:00:55.070 --> 00:00:56.610
and we knocked out a podcast.

22
00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:57.477
Right, we did a podcast

23
00:00:57.477 --> 00:00:59.790
and then I did a little shooting

24
00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:02.520
with you for the documentary with Cal Fussman.

25
00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:03.580
What happened to that documentary?

26
00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:06.840
It's done and off the editing room floor,

27
00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:10.540
however you'd say it, and getting ready to be pushed out.

28
00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:11.373
What's it called?

29
00:01:11.373 --> 00:01:14.081
"What The Fuck is Your Exit Strategy?"

30
00:01:14.081 --> 00:01:16.890
And the idea is, and it probably,

31
00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:18.440
I know it pisses you off.

32
00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:20.870
What are people thinking when they don't eat healthy,

33
00:01:20.870 --> 00:01:22.440
when they don't act healthy?

34
00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:23.580
And so like what,

35
00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:25.410
like we talk about exit strategy in business.

36
00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:28.248
What's your exit strategy from the planet?

37
00:01:28.248 --> 00:01:31.030
Do you plan on living in a hospital for the last 20 years?

38
00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:33.930
And that title came about because Cal,

39
00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:34.910
a mutual friend of ours,

40
00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:38.640
Cal Fussman famous writer invited me

41
00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:40.630
to breakfast with Larry King one day.

42
00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:41.862
And so I'm sitting there-

43
00:01:41.862 --> 00:01:42.695
At Nate 'n Al's?

44
00:01:42.695 --> 00:01:47.160
Yeah, so I'm having a salad and Larry is pouring.

45
00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:50.827
I mean, pouring sugar all over this bowl of cereal.

46
00:01:50.827 --> 00:01:53.420
"Ah kid, what are you doing with the salad?"

47
00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:54.730
And he goes, "I eat like this.

48
00:01:54.730 --> 00:01:57.677
I got a colonic every day," and I just thought,

49
00:01:57.677 --> 00:01:59.984
"Fuck, what's the exit strategy?"

50
00:01:59.984 --> 00:02:02.110
(laughing)

51
00:02:02.110 --> 00:02:05.370
And it came from there, so is Cal, like the protagonist,

52
00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:07.650
he's who were like taking this adventure with?

53
00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:09.220
Yeah, we took the adventure with him

54
00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:11.053
and he was impossible to deal with.

55
00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:14.820
And unbeknownst to me, you know, 20 years earlier,

56
00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:16.100
he did it maybe 30 years earlier,

57
00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:18.183
he did it with Jack LaLanne,

58
00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:21.170
and I think Cal was really good

59
00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:23.690
for an article or for film,

60
00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:26.220
but then like most people falls off the wagon

61
00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:29.740
when there's no reason to be taking care of yourself.

62
00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:32.620
I just remember him being sort of obstinate

63
00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:34.640
in the interview, like pushing back

64
00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:36.010
and it's like, come on, man,

65
00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:38.290
what do you, what do you, you know, get on board here.

66
00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:41.290
Listen, I fought my in-laws when I met my wife,

67
00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:43.270
my mother, by the way, my mother in the 70s

68
00:02:43.270 --> 00:02:44.510
was into yoga, meditate,

69
00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:45.900
all the stuff that we're into.

70
00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:47.860
And she fought the whole world.

71
00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:50.870
And so there were no Whole Foods or yoga journals back then.

72
00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:54.842
So I watched the pushback and, and it's, I mean,

73
00:02:54.842 --> 00:02:56.230
maybe not in California,

74
00:02:56.230 --> 00:03:00.060
but the pushback is massive everywhere to this idea

75
00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:01.830
of just take care of yourself.

76
00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:06.510
I mean, look at COVID in the US versus everywhere else.

77
00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:08.810
Mm. Yeah. I wanted to ask you.

78
00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:12.353
I mean, it's gotta be brutal right now for you with Spartan.

79
00:03:12.353 --> 00:03:15.253
How are you keeping this thing from capsizing?

80
00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:18.070
You know, when it first happened,

81
00:03:18.070 --> 00:03:20.080
I became friends two years ago,

82
00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:22.720
skiing with the owner of Saks fifth Avenue,

83
00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:24.120
he owns Hudson Bay company,

84
00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:26.130
which is the equivalent of Saks in Canada,

85
00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:27.610
Saks Fifth Avenue.

86
00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:29.620
Incredibly successful guy.

87
00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:32.590
And when it was all going down, I was with Gerard Butler.

88
00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:34.010
Check this out, I was with Gerard Butler,

89
00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:36.600
I was in Sparta, Greece for the 2,500-year anniversary

90
00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:38.670
of Thermopylae, the movie was based

91
00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:40.620
on the battle of Thermopylae.

92
00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:43.610
And Trump closes the border,

93
00:03:43.610 --> 00:03:46.330
and at that moment it became very real.

94
00:03:46.330 --> 00:03:49.810
Because up until then, we had closed a few races in China,

95
00:03:49.810 --> 00:03:51.040
had closed a few races in Japan,

96
00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:52.200
but it was like no big deal,

97
00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:54.410
this thing will be over in a jiffy.

98
00:03:54.410 --> 00:03:58.970
And I'm in Sparta and the country gets shut down

99
00:03:58.970 --> 00:04:01.750
and I call my friend from Saks and I'm like, what the fuck?

100
00:04:01.750 --> 00:04:05.110
And he says, he goes, "You gotta preserve cash.

101
00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:06.260
You gotta preserve cash,

102
00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:09.280
you gotta protect the employees that you can,

103
00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:12.380
and you got to stay in touch with your customers."

104
00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:16.450
And thank God I had that phone call on March 13th, 14th,

105
00:04:16.450 --> 00:04:19.430
because I would have just like,

106
00:04:19.430 --> 00:04:21.805
I mean we had 501 people working for us.

107
00:04:21.805 --> 00:04:25.270
I got 325 events shut down overnight around the world,

108
00:04:25.270 --> 00:04:28.200
and you can't keep paying people and bills

109
00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:32.289
and all these things and lawyers and,

110
00:04:32.289 --> 00:04:33.122
you know better than anybody,

111
00:04:33.122 --> 00:04:36.280
the lawyers and not have revenue coming in.

112
00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:40.240
So, you know, I'm ashamed to say,

113
00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:43.200
I thought, I didn't listen to the advice of him,

114
00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:44.300
I didn't listen to the advice of my friends

115
00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:45.570
that run hedge funds.

116
00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:49.890
I thought come July 1, we're back. The world is back.

117
00:04:49.890 --> 00:04:51.620
Well, we've never seen anything like this

118
00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:52.570
in our lifetimes.

119
00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:54.360
There was no reason to think that we wouldn't be able

120
00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:57.330
to get over the hump and get back to some level of normal.

121
00:04:57.330 --> 00:04:58.170
And I'm an optimist.

122
00:04:58.170 --> 00:05:00.680
I mean our whole business is based on optimism, right?

123
00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:04.150
And I just thought, yeah, well, here we are.

124
00:05:04.150 --> 00:05:06.100
So did you have to lay off a bunch of people?

125
00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:08.113
350 people furloughed.

126
00:05:09.269 --> 00:05:11.019
And I think we just did a few more.

127
00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:14.670
Silver lining in all of this is we became

128
00:05:14.670 --> 00:05:17.130
a lot more efficient as an organization.

129
00:05:17.130 --> 00:05:19.260
There were a lot of deficits in our company

130
00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:24.260
that we never tended to; technology, CRM, e-com,

131
00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:25.173
go down the list,

132
00:05:25.173 --> 00:05:26.610
things that we just didn't have time for,

133
00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:28.450
putting on 325 events.

134
00:05:28.450 --> 00:05:32.110
And so we're becoming much better in those areas,

135
00:05:32.110 --> 00:05:34.130
but it's really a race against the clock.

136
00:05:34.130 --> 00:05:37.360
It's, you know, if we're back on next year

137
00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:40.373
and we're allowed to put on events, it's gonna be great.

138
00:05:41.319 --> 00:05:44.720
But, I just don't know.

139
00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:48.130
I mean, China, two weeks ago had 10,000 kids

140
00:05:48.130 --> 00:05:50.140
at a Chinese event in China.

141
00:05:50.140 --> 00:05:51.550
China's back in business.

142
00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:53.377
They got vaccines that cost $30 USD.

143
00:05:53.377 --> 00:05:55.700
I mean I could go up to the corner store

144
00:05:55.700 --> 00:05:57.063
and get a shot in our arm.

145
00:05:58.580 --> 00:05:59.690
But outside of that...

146
00:05:59.690 --> 00:06:02.410
Yeah, so you can ramp up in China.

147
00:06:02.410 --> 00:06:05.260
I could ramp up in China, but the US

148
00:06:05.260 --> 00:06:07.430
was 50% of our business.

149
00:06:07.430 --> 00:06:10.650
Europe was 25% of our business, right?

150
00:06:10.650 --> 00:06:14.230
So China does not offset, but it gives us hope

151
00:06:14.230 --> 00:06:16.530
that if China's back and they were three or four months

152
00:06:16.530 --> 00:06:17.730
before us in shutting down,

153
00:06:17.730 --> 00:06:19.550
maybe three or four months from now we'll be back.

154
00:06:19.550 --> 00:06:21.330
Yeah. We'll see.

155
00:06:21.330 --> 00:06:22.450
We'll see.
Who knows.

156
00:06:22.450 --> 00:06:24.388
Who fucking knows.
It's craziness.

157
00:06:24.388 --> 00:06:25.550
It's craziness.
I know, it's so crazy.

158
00:06:25.550 --> 00:06:27.170
Well, what I've noticed in you

159
00:06:27.170 --> 00:06:31.160
just from a distance is this kind of pivot

160
00:06:31.160 --> 00:06:35.240
into really a media company, like a content platform,

161
00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:37.250
like suddenly you're vlogging all the time

162
00:06:37.250 --> 00:06:39.550
and suddenly there's videos from you

163
00:06:39.550 --> 00:06:41.512
like every single day.

164
00:06:41.512 --> 00:06:44.504
I know, I've gone a little nuts. I apologize for that.

165
00:06:44.504 --> 00:06:46.280
We locked down on the farm in Vermont.

166
00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:47.550
That's where this whole thing was started.

167
00:06:47.550 --> 00:06:51.330
My wife and I had our four children,

168
00:06:51.330 --> 00:06:53.770
I don't know how to say that politically correctly.

169
00:06:53.770 --> 00:06:56.380
She birthed four kids on the farm.

170
00:06:56.380 --> 00:06:58.113
I think you're allowed to say that.

171
00:06:58.113 --> 00:06:59.910
Yeah, in Vermont, along we had cows

172
00:06:59.910 --> 00:07:01.120
and chickens and goats.

173
00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:03.938
And that's where Spartan was started.

174
00:07:03.938 --> 00:07:07.650
As soon as I got back from the Thermopylae thing

175
00:07:07.650 --> 00:07:10.968
I just described in Sparta, Greece, landed on the farm,

176
00:07:10.968 --> 00:07:14.483
holed up with an Olympic wrestler.

177
00:07:14.483 --> 00:07:16.636
That's another whole story.

178
00:07:16.636 --> 00:07:20.950
A video team, a bunch of people on the Spartan team,

179
00:07:20.950 --> 00:07:24.120
trainers and so forth, 12, 13 of us.

180
00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:25.580
And we just started filming every day

181
00:07:25.580 --> 00:07:27.510
because there was nothing else to do.

182
00:07:27.510 --> 00:07:30.900
Like preserve your cash, protect your employees

183
00:07:30.900 --> 00:07:32.520
and stay in touch with your customers.

184
00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:34.270
So just film, film, film, film, film,

185
00:07:34.270 --> 00:07:37.530
because I like to work and we couldn't put on events.

186
00:07:37.530 --> 00:07:39.330
And so that's what we did.

187
00:07:39.330 --> 00:07:40.163
Yeah, and you keep the brand strong

188
00:07:40.163 --> 00:07:43.780
and you keep people excited about what Spartan is,

189
00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:45.850
and they hear it from you directly.

190
00:07:45.850 --> 00:07:48.120
Yeah. So just lots of filming.

191
00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:51.930
But as you know, it drains your energy levels because you,

192
00:07:51.930 --> 00:07:54.440
like right before we just started this podcast,

193
00:07:54.440 --> 00:07:56.000
anybody listening or watching,

194
00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:57.390
Rich and I were talking regularly.

195
00:07:57.390 --> 00:08:00.060
As soon as the filming started, you perked up,

196
00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:00.910
your chest came out.
Did I?

197
00:08:00.910 --> 00:08:03.255
Yeah, and you were like on.

198
00:08:03.255 --> 00:08:04.309
I'm trying to be normal, man.

199
00:08:04.309 --> 00:08:05.193
I want this to be as relaxed as possible-

200
00:08:05.193 --> 00:08:06.820
No, but we both did it.

201
00:08:06.820 --> 00:08:08.430
You don't even notice you're doing it, right?

202
00:08:08.430 --> 00:08:10.810
So it just sucks energy out of you.

203
00:08:10.810 --> 00:08:12.660
Well, I just noticed, like, this is,

204
00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:14.480
I think this is the fifth podcast I've done

205
00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:15.580
in the last seven days.

206
00:08:15.580 --> 00:08:18.240
We're on a bit of a tear and it's draining, man.

207
00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:21.766
Like I, you know, I don't know like how Rogan does,

208
00:08:21.766 --> 00:08:23.221
you know, four or five of these things a week.

209
00:08:23.221 --> 00:08:24.054
Maybe it's cause he doesn't prepare.

210
00:08:24.054 --> 00:08:27.200
But like, I prepare, like I put a lot of energy into it.

211
00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:28.950
I want it to be this amazing experience.

212
00:08:28.950 --> 00:08:31.440
And when it's done, it's kind of like an athletic event.

213
00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:33.690
Then I go home and I crash.

214
00:08:33.690 --> 00:08:35.990
And now like, yeah, it's like,

215
00:08:35.990 --> 00:08:36.890
it is its own kind of endurance thing.

216
00:08:36.890 --> 00:08:39.220
And it does take, even though it's not physical,

217
00:08:39.220 --> 00:08:42.510
there's an emotional, like energy toll that it takes.

218
00:08:42.510 --> 00:08:44.090
It'd be easier, I think you'd agree with this.

219
00:08:44.090 --> 00:08:46.330
It would be easier to go for a run.

220
00:08:46.330 --> 00:08:47.876
Oh, for sure.

221
00:08:47.876 --> 00:08:48.709
Right?
Yeah.

222
00:08:48.709 --> 00:08:49.542
And your mind is working,

223
00:08:49.542 --> 00:08:52.030
but your body, you're breathing, you're sweating.

224
00:08:52.030 --> 00:08:53.740
Also I can control that, you know.

225
00:08:53.740 --> 00:08:56.370
With this, it's sort of like, there's an aspect

226
00:08:56.370 --> 00:08:58.560
of trying to, you know, have guard rails up

227
00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:00.190
and direct it in a certain way.

228
00:09:00.190 --> 00:09:02.400
But at the same time you have to like, let go

229
00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:04.040
and allow it to be what it wants to be.

230
00:09:04.040 --> 00:09:05.570
So there's a vulnerability with that

231
00:09:05.570 --> 00:09:08.460
that's different from going out and pushing yourself

232
00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:10.000
where it's just you and you.

233
00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:11.520
No doubt.

234
00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:13.130
And so, I just went through, you know,

235
00:09:13.130 --> 00:09:15.750
let's call it 120 days straight of filming

236
00:09:15.750 --> 00:09:17.570
on the farm every day.

237
00:09:17.570 --> 00:09:19.790
And something cool happened this week,

238
00:09:19.790 --> 00:09:21.930
and you might not know about is once we realized,

239
00:09:21.930 --> 00:09:23.330
hey, this is going to go on longer than we thought,

240
00:09:23.330 --> 00:09:25.010
it's gonna be much more painful than we thought,

241
00:09:25.010 --> 00:09:27.180
we got to lay off more people, I said, you know,

242
00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:29.840
we need an event, we need one event.

243
00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:33.070
Something to show our community, seven million people

244
00:09:33.070 --> 00:09:36.760
that like, we're still here and shit's still happening.

245
00:09:36.760 --> 00:09:39.913
So this past weekend we brought 12 women, 12 men

246
00:09:39.913 --> 00:09:44.913
from CrossFit, from triathlon, from the NFL, Spartan.

247
00:09:45.470 --> 00:09:48.220
And we brought them all together to find out

248
00:09:48.220 --> 00:09:50.526
who's the fittest of them all.

249
00:09:50.526 --> 00:09:52.770
And my team came up with a whole thing.

250
00:09:52.770 --> 00:09:54.650
I can't even tell you all the names of the athletes

251
00:09:54.650 --> 00:09:56.600
that were there, because my team organized,

252
00:09:56.600 --> 00:09:58.422
I just wanted to have a Spartan event.

253
00:09:58.422 --> 00:10:00.280
And they came up with this whole thing.

254
00:10:00.280 --> 00:10:03.380
They actually wouldn't allow me back on my own farm

255
00:10:03.380 --> 00:10:06.005
because I needed another COVID test.

256
00:10:06.005 --> 00:10:07.710
So here I was, outside my own farm

257
