And say, "Hey, I need a favor."

516
00:20:26.300 --> 00:20:28.390
Right.
And then you're in it.

517
00:20:28.390 --> 00:20:31.030
Once they do that favor for you though, that's-

518
00:20:31.030 --> 00:20:33.930
And technically because he got me in the business, like,

519
00:20:35.237 --> 00:20:37.180
I mean I couldn't say no to anything.

520
00:20:37.180 --> 00:20:38.730
Right.
Right?

521
00:20:38.730 --> 00:20:41.360
Did you call upon those guys to do some favors for you?

522
00:20:41.360 --> 00:20:42.890
And then did you find it difficult

523
00:20:42.890 --> 00:20:45.300
to then extricate yourself from the whole thing?

524
00:20:45.300 --> 00:20:49.720
Well, so yes, I asked for favors often

525
00:20:49.720 --> 00:20:51.370
because people owed me money.

526
00:20:51.370 --> 00:20:54.040
And I remember saying to Joe once,

527
00:20:54.040 --> 00:20:55.520
a guy owed me $60,000.

528
00:20:55.520 --> 00:20:58.750
And he said, bring this bottle of champagne

529
00:20:58.750 --> 00:21:01.783
over to his house and just let him know it came from me.

530
00:21:02.800 --> 00:21:05.480
And so I go over to the house, I don't ask for the money.

531
00:21:05.480 --> 00:21:09.140
I just say, "Hey, Joe asked me to drop this off for you."

532
00:21:09.140 --> 00:21:10.580
And he literally goes over to the wall,

533
00:21:10.580 --> 00:21:12.610
takes a painting down, opens the safe, like in a movie,

534
00:21:12.610 --> 00:21:15.402
grabbed 60,000 in cash and hands it to me.

535
00:21:15.402 --> 00:21:18.750
I didn't even...
(Rich laughing)

536
00:21:18.750 --> 00:21:23.110
And there were a few incidences like that.

537
00:21:23.110 --> 00:21:24.650
Now, to the second part of the question,

538
00:21:24.650 --> 00:21:26.890
did you find yourself beholden?

539
00:21:26.890 --> 00:21:31.890
I guess I got lucky in the sense that Giuliani came to be,

540
00:21:32.110 --> 00:21:34.711
and then one by one, all these guys,

541
00:21:34.711 --> 00:21:36.280
(talking over each other)

542
00:21:36.280 --> 00:21:37.113
they all started to go to jail.

543
00:21:37.113 --> 00:21:42.030
And so, I didn't have a long tail in, like it was good.

544
00:21:42.030 --> 00:21:43.769
Right, right, right.

545
00:21:43.769 --> 00:21:44.602
I didn't know it at the time.

546
00:21:44.602 --> 00:21:47.380
So where does Cornell come into the whole thing?

547
00:21:47.380 --> 00:21:49.832
Like kind of in the middle of the pool business, right?

548
00:21:49.832 --> 00:21:50.665
Yeah, so I'm running this business.

549
00:21:50.665 --> 00:21:52.560
I'm feeling great, it's growing.

550
00:21:52.560 --> 00:21:56.520
I buy a backhoe and a Bobcat and I got a building.

551
00:21:56.520 --> 00:21:58.910
And like, like it's a real business, right?

552
00:21:58.910 --> 00:21:59.830
My mom's living in Ithaca.

553
00:21:59.830 --> 00:22:02.485
So I have to spend a lot of time in Ithaca,

554
00:22:02.485 --> 00:22:03.318
that's where I live.

555
00:22:03.318 --> 00:22:04.500
Going back and forth, running the business,

556
00:22:04.500 --> 00:22:07.063
by my dad's house, going back to mom.

557
00:22:07.063 --> 00:22:11.660
And I'm leaving high school in Ithaca,

558
00:22:11.660 --> 00:22:12.900
going back to run my business,

559
00:22:12.900 --> 00:22:14.130
no intention of going to college.

560
00:22:14.130 --> 00:22:16.430
I didn't have like most families where they bring you

561
00:22:16.430 --> 00:22:18.020
on a college tour.

562
00:22:18.020 --> 00:22:19.900
Even though I lived in Ithaca where Cornell was,

563
00:22:19.900 --> 00:22:22.010
no one told me anything about school,

564
00:22:22.010 --> 00:22:24.100
and my grades weren't that good.

565
00:22:24.100 --> 00:22:28.940
And my friend says to me, "Hey, why don't we go to Cornell?"

566
00:22:28.940 --> 00:22:31.677
I'm two months from graduating high school,

567
00:22:31.677 --> 00:22:33.990
and I say, "Well, how would we go to Cornell?"

568
00:22:33.990 --> 00:22:37.000
And he said, "My dad's a professor. He'll get us in."

569
00:22:37.000 --> 00:22:39.770
And that made perfect sense to me because I grew up

570
00:22:39.770 --> 00:22:42.190
in the neighborhood where you'd have a guy

571
00:22:42.190 --> 00:22:44.440
and he would get you into a restaurant or whatever.

572
00:22:44.440 --> 00:22:47.530
So this is like, yeah, we got us in college.

573
00:22:47.530 --> 00:22:50.540
So we both get dressed up, we do our interviews,

574
00:22:50.540 --> 00:22:52.060
we do everything we're supposed to do.

575
00:22:52.060 --> 00:22:53.940
It's gonna be a shoe in.

576
00:22:53.940 --> 00:22:55.860
and neither of us get accepted.

577
00:22:55.860 --> 00:22:58.660
And now I was intrigued like, oh,

578
00:22:58.660 --> 00:23:00.260
we have a guy, we can't get in,

579
00:23:00.260 --> 00:23:03.280
and now I'm intrigued in this thing.

580
00:23:03.280 --> 00:23:05.610
Tell me I can't, now I gotta do it.

581
00:23:05.610 --> 00:23:06.933
Where does that come from?

582
00:23:07.770 --> 00:23:10.300
'Cause that's just born and bred into you.

583
00:23:10.300 --> 00:23:11.930
Probably a neighborhood thing.

584
00:23:11.930 --> 00:23:14.240
Like when we, let's go back to your conversation

585
00:23:14.240 --> 00:23:16.490
about resiliency and grit and those athletes.

586
00:23:17.820 --> 00:23:22.210
In this neighborhood, these guys went to jail,

587
00:23:22.210 --> 00:23:27.050
they got killed, and that was just part of the job, right?

588
00:23:27.050 --> 00:23:29.270
And then if you were running a somewhat legal business,

589
00:23:29.270 --> 00:23:31.060
you owned the local pizza place,

590
00:23:31.060 --> 00:23:34.740
or a trucking company or whatever, you hustled.

591
00:23:34.740 --> 00:23:37.330
You were up at 5:00 AM, the diesel trucks were running,

592
00:23:37.330 --> 00:23:39.790
the pizza place was second generation, third generation.

593
00:23:39.790 --> 00:23:41.858
They were making, you know what I mean?

594
00:23:41.858 --> 00:23:43.810
Like everybody just hustled.

595
00:23:43.810 --> 00:23:46.670
So probably comes from that.

596
00:23:46.670 --> 00:23:50.280
It probably comes from just seeing this, like, you know,

597
00:23:50.280 --> 00:23:52.310
fuck that, I'm gonna get this done.

598
00:23:52.310 --> 00:23:55.833
Kinda like, there's no no, right?

599
00:23:56.743 --> 00:23:57.900
Right, so you get the no.

600
00:23:57.900 --> 00:24:00.197
We get the no, and now I wanna go,

601
00:24:00.197 --> 00:24:02.160
and I'm debating going back to run my business.

602
00:24:02.160 --> 00:24:04.170
And my friend says, "Listen, my dad just said,

603
00:24:04.170 --> 00:24:08.137
we can go extramural, we could take three classes each.

604
00:24:08.137 --> 00:24:11.480
When everybody else gets matriculated into Cornell,

605
00:24:11.480 --> 00:24:12.670
we can take three classes each.

606
00:24:12.670 --> 00:24:15.390
They'll do five, we're not official,

607
00:24:15.390 --> 00:24:18.150
but if we do well, they have to let us in."

608
00:24:18.150 --> 00:24:21.420
Logical, so I said, okay, you know what we'll do then?

609
00:24:21.420 --> 00:24:22.450
I said, when I go back to Queens

610
00:24:22.450 --> 00:24:25.710
to run my businesses this summer, I'll go to St. John's.

611
00:24:25.710 --> 00:24:27.987
It's in my route anyway and all the pools I clean

612
00:24:27.987 --> 00:24:29.339
and the work I'll do.

613
00:24:29.339 --> 00:24:30.800
I'll stop in St. John's, I'll take a couple of classes.

614
00:24:30.800 --> 00:24:32.230
I'll learn how to study.

615
00:24:32.230 --> 00:24:36.200
And then when I'm going to Cornell, if we get in,

616
00:24:36.200 --> 00:24:38.140
I'll have gotten my five classes done,

617
00:24:38.140 --> 00:24:40.450
I won't be behind the other kids.

618
00:24:40.450 --> 00:24:43.210
My friend says to me, "That's ridiculous.

619
00:24:43.210 --> 00:24:46.080
If we're gonna hustle in September at Cornell,

620
00:24:46.080 --> 00:24:48.500
why don't we go to Vegas all summer and party?"

621
00:24:48.500 --> 00:24:49.400
Right.
Right?

622
00:24:49.400 --> 00:24:50.990
And that was a divergence right there,

623
00:24:50.990 --> 00:24:53.160
and that was a real big lesson for me

624
00:24:53.160 --> 00:24:54.810
on like delaying gratification.

625
00:24:54.810 --> 00:24:56.860
So he heads to Vegas, I head to St. John's.

626
00:24:56.860 --> 00:24:59.180
I run my business. We both meet up in September.

627
00:24:59.180 --> 00:25:00.678
We do our three classes.

628
00:25:00.678 --> 00:25:02.610
We do very, very well.

629
00:25:02.610 --> 00:25:03.560
I get two A's and a B.

630
00:25:03.560 --> 00:25:06.029
And for me at an Ivy league school, two A's,

631
00:25:06.029 --> 00:25:07.520
I never got two A's ever.

632
00:25:07.520 --> 00:25:09.730
And reapply.

633
00:25:09.730 --> 00:25:11.730
Definitely getting in now.
They don't accept us.

634
00:25:11.730 --> 00:25:13.214
Right.

635
00:25:13.214 --> 00:25:15.390
And now I'm even more invested.

636
00:25:15.390 --> 00:25:17.560
I did my classes at St. John's.

637
00:25:17.560 --> 00:25:21.480
I just studied my ass off, and they didn't accept me.

638
00:25:21.480 --> 00:25:23.937
And the woman, the admissions woman says to me,

639
00:25:23.937 --> 00:25:27.570
"Listen, we'd have everybody and their brother,

640
00:25:27.570 --> 00:25:29.730
you know, trying to get in here if this was allowed.

641
00:25:29.730 --> 00:25:31.370
You're gonna have to go to another school,

642
00:25:31.370 --> 00:25:33.610
and then after three years at like Ithaca College

643
00:25:33.610 --> 00:25:36.040
or somewhere else, then, then apply."

644
00:25:36.040 --> 00:25:37.420
And I was like, "Maybe you didn't hear me.

645
00:25:37.420 --> 00:25:39.517
I'm gonna go here."

646
00:25:39.517 --> 00:25:41.160
(both laugh)

647
00:25:41.160 --> 00:25:42.930
So I'm gonna do it again.

648
00:25:42.930 --> 00:25:44.739
Then she's gotta let me in.

649
00:25:44.739 --> 00:25:46.420
And so he diverts, my friend taps out-

650
00:25:46.420 --> 00:25:47.560
Does he go back to Vegas?

651
00:25:47.560 --> 00:25:49.610
He goes back to Vegas, he goes to UNLV.

652
00:25:50.606 --> 00:25:54.650
And I go back, do another semester, do well, reapply.

653
00:25:54.650 --> 00:25:57.470
They don't accept me. Do another semester. I do well.

654
00:25:57.470 --> 00:25:58.303
Don't accept me.

655
00:25:58.303 --> 00:26:00.980
My fourth semester, I'm broken.

656
00:26:00.980 --> 00:26:02.633
I finally, I hit my limit.

657
00:26:03.870 --> 00:26:05.190
This is your own death race.

658
00:26:05.190 --> 00:26:06.160
Yeah, it's my own death race.

659
00:26:06.160 --> 00:26:08.410
And I'm just feeling like my business is doing well.

660
00:26:08.410 --> 00:26:11.005
I got all these, like, I don't need this.

661
00:26:11.005 --> 00:26:12.626
I don't need the headache.

662
00:26:12.626 --> 00:26:15.120
And I tell my mom, you know, I'm gonna pack it in.

663
00:26:15.120 --> 00:26:17.070
And she was probably bragging for four semesters,

664
00:26:17.070 --> 00:26:18.100
her son was going to Cornell,

665
00:26:18.100 --> 00:26:21.730
even though I technically was not a Cornell student.

666
00:26:21.730 --> 00:26:24.150
And she says, go meet my yoga,

667
00:26:24.150 --> 00:26:27.100
she teaches yoga, my mom at this point in the living room.

668
00:26:27.100 --> 00:26:28.960
And she says, go meet my yoga student,

669
00:26:28.960 --> 00:26:30.773
Professor Anita Rasin.

670
00:26:32.412 --> 00:26:34.126
She might be able to help you, I don't know.

671
00:26:34.126 --> 00:26:36.470
And my mom was the last person I would have asked,

672
00:26:36.470 --> 00:26:37.520
like for a connection.

673
00:26:37.520 --> 00:26:39.380
'Cause she was crunchy and Bohemian

674
00:26:39.380 --> 00:26:41.480
and my dad would have had the contact.

675
00:26:41.480 --> 00:26:43.470
So anyway, out of respect to my mom,

676
00:26:43.470 --> 00:26:46.280
I go meet professor Anita Rasin,

677
00:26:46.280 --> 00:26:48.846
she sits me down, looks at my grades,

678
00:26:48.846 --> 00:26:50.350
asked me a bunch of questions.

679
00:26:50.350 --> 00:26:52.038
She says, you know, I run a department

680
00:26:52.038 --> 00:26:56.280
within one of the schools at Cornell, textiles.

681
00:26:56.280 --> 00:26:58.010
We study textiles.

682
00:26:58.010 --> 00:27:02.110
And there's 92 women in the department, there's no men.

683
00:27:02.110 --> 00:27:03.080
Do you like textiles?

684
00:27:03.080 --> 00:27:05.000
I was like, "I love textiles."

685
00:27:05.000 --> 00:27:06.420
I didn't even know what a textile was.

686
00:27:06.420 --> 00:27:07.253
Like I'm in.

687
00:27:08.320 --> 00:27:11.230
And so she finally accepts me and I ended up

688
00:27:11.230 --> 00:27:15.430
spending the rest of my time at Cornell studying textiles,

689
00:27:15.430 --> 00:27:17.850
which if I had to do it again, I would do,

690
00:27:17.850 --> 00:27:21.350
because I could watch any movie from any era

691
00:27:21.350 --> 00:27:24.670
and tell you based on hemlines, what time-

692
00:27:24.670 --> 00:27:26.520
That's amazing.
Yeah, when that happened.

693
00:27:26.520 --> 00:27:28.460
What years were you at Cornell then?

694
00:27:28.460 --> 00:27:31.483
I entered in '86, graduated in '90.

695
00:27:32.974 --> 00:27:34.140
Aha, so you left right before I got there

696
00:27:34.140 --> 00:27:35.420
'cause I went to law school at Cornell.

697
00:27:35.420 --> 00:27:37.399
Okay, law school is beautiful.

698
00:27:37.399 --> 00:27:39.692
I was there '92 to '94,

699
00:27:39.692 --> 00:27:40.525
so you were out there.

700
00:27:40.525 --> 00:27:41.540
But then you got in legitimately.

701
00:27:41.540 --> 00:27:43.400
You didn't need to pull strings.

702
00:27:43.400 --> 00:27:45.650
No, well, legitimately sort of,

703
00:27:45.650 --> 00:27:48.740
I was the last person admitted to the law school class

704
00:27:48.740 --> 00:27:49.953
off the wait list.

705
00:27:50.809 --> 00:27:52.500
So I showed up, like,

706
00:27:52.500 --> 00:27:53.950
I didn't think I was going to law school.

707
00:27:53.950 --> 00:27:56.300
I got in at the last minute, I was living in New York city

708
00:27:56.300 --> 00:27:58.610
and packed a bag and tried to figure out where Ithaca was

709
00:27:58.610 --> 00:27:59.810
and showed up.

710
00:27:59.810 --> 00:28:01.620
And everybody had been studying all summer.

711
00:28:01.620 --> 00:28:03.290
And I was like, what am I even doing here?

712
00:28:03.290 --> 00:28:05.150
'Cause I'd already decided I wasn't gonna go,

713
00:28:05.150 --> 00:28:07.470
and it was like a very spontaneous decision.

714
00:28:07.470 --> 00:28:09.320
And you liked it when you arrived?

715
00:28:09.320 --> 00:28:10.860
I mean, I liked being,

716
00:28:10.860 --> 00:28:12.810
I was getting out of control in New York.

717
00:28:12.810 --> 00:28:15.080
So I liked being in a structured environment

718
00:28:15.080 --> 00:28:17.433
and I like school and the law school,

719
00:28:17.433 --> 00:28:19.390
it's kind of like "The Shining" there though,

720
00:28:19.390 --> 00:28:21.370
because it's so fucking cold.

721
00:28:21.370 --> 00:28:23.230
And you're all in this one building

722
00:28:23.230 --> 00:28:25.060
and a lot of the 1Ls live in a dorm

723
00:28:25.060 --> 00:28:26.550
that's attached to the law school.

724
00:28:26.550 --> 00:28:27.973
So they never go outside.

725
00:28:28.910 --> 00:28:31.600
So it gets a little weird and incestuous,

726
00:28:31.600 --> 00:28:34.623
but I basically just spent most of my time at Rulloff's.

727
00:28:36.360 --> 00:28:39.504
I bar backed at Rulloff's.

728
00:28:39.504 --> 00:28:40.337
Oh, you did?

729
00:28:40.337 --> 00:28:41.530
I mean, I was there like four nights a week.

730
00:28:41.530 --> 00:28:43.230
I was gone by the time you got there.

731
00:28:43.230 --> 00:28:45.433
But I think I made $16 a week.

732
00:28:46.450 --> 00:28:48.170
That's tough, when you're making

733
00:28:48.170 --> 00:28:51.620
like mid-six figures or whatever you were making

734
00:28:51.620 --> 00:28:52.480
in the pool business.

735
00:28:52.480 --> 00:28:54.513
Exactly, but it was good for me to,

736
00:28:55.382 --> 00:28:57.060
it was a way from me to mingle and meet other people

737
00:28:57.060 --> 00:28:58.240
while I, I like to work.

738
00:28:58.240 --> 00:29:01.560
Obviously you're hearing that in my story.

739
00:29:01.560 --> 00:29:03.710
And rather than wasting time just drinking,

740
00:29:04.700 --> 00:29:06.870
which is the way I looked at it.

741
00:29:06.870 --> 00:29:10.060
I was around it while I was working, getting paid.

742
00:29:10.060 --> 00:29:12.600
I could have used you in my orbit back then, man.

743
00:29:12.600 --> 00:29:15.300
I needed a little discipline at the time.

744
00:29:15.300 --> 00:29:17.360
Cool, so then you get out of Cornell,

745
00:29:17.360 --> 00:29:19.393
but then it's back to the pool business?

746
00:29:20.707 --> 00:29:22.840
So first of all, anybody listening

747
00:29:22.840 --> 00:29:24.947
that doesn't know Cornell and correct me

748
00:29:24.947 --> 00:29:26.120
if I'm wrong, Rich.

749
00:29:26.120 --> 00:29:29.270
But the law school to me feels most like Cambridge

750
00:29:29.270 --> 00:29:31.390
or Harry Potter, at least from the outside,

751
00:29:31.390 --> 00:29:32.923
it has that look, right?

752
00:29:34.470 --> 00:29:37.670
So I'm about to graduate on time,

753
00:29:37.670 --> 00:29:40.170
which was unbelievable because I was so far

754
00:29:40.170 --> 00:29:42.500
behind from those four semesters.

755
00:29:42.500 --> 00:29:46.550
And I take professor Ben Daniel's class

756
00:29:46.550 --> 00:29:50.640
at the Graduate Management School, MBA program.

757
00:29:50.640 --> 00:29:52.430
Right, you didn't combine it, like-

758
00:29:52.430 --> 00:29:53.670
I didn't do the combined.

759
00:29:53.670 --> 00:29:55.778
They didn't accept me for the combined.

760
00:29:55.778 --> 00:29:57.300
It was just, I clearly was not smart enough

761
00:29:57.300 --> 00:29:59.150
for this whole thing, but I was allowed

762
00:29:59.150 --> 00:29:59.983
to take a class there.

763
00:29:59.983 --> 00:30:02.050
A lot of people took the entrepreneurship class

764
00:30:02.050 --> 00:30:03.720
at that school.

765
00:30:03.720 --> 00:30:07.689
And as part of it, we all came up with a business plan

766
00:30:07.689 --> 00:30:11.550
and we presented that business plan to a panel of judges.

767
00:30:11.550 --> 00:30:16.400
And I won, I won the $5,000 award

768
00:30:16.400 --> 00:30:19.930
that came from the owners of Riunite Wines.

769
00:30:19.930 --> 00:30:22.027
They were awarding every semester,

770
00:30:22.027 --> 00:30:25.310
$5,000 to the best idea, best presentation.

771
00:30:25.310 --> 00:30:29.800
And the judge, one of the judges was an Italian guy,

772
00:30:29.800 --> 00:30:31.830
