But you took it to such an insane level.

1023
00:40:11.390 --> 00:40:13.713
I mean, there's that one,

1024
00:40:13.713 --> 00:40:16.060
there was like a one like four-day, five-day period

1025
00:40:16.060 --> 00:40:20.040
you did the Vermont 100, 100-mile run.

1026
00:40:20.040 --> 00:40:22.910
Then you get on a plane and you fly to Vegas

1027
00:40:22.910 --> 00:40:25.500
and drive to Badwater, and you do Badwater,

1028
00:40:25.500 --> 00:40:28.740
Badwater's a race we talk about all the time on the podcast.

1029
00:40:28.740 --> 00:40:31.750
Then you go to a wedding, and then you go to Lake Placid

1030
00:40:31.750 --> 00:40:34.310
and do the Ironman, in what, like four days?

1031
00:40:34.310 --> 00:40:35.260
You did your research, yeah.

1032
00:40:35.260 --> 00:40:36.968
Five days or something like that?

1033
00:40:36.968 --> 00:40:39.280
The hardest part of that whole week was the wedding.

1034
00:40:39.280 --> 00:40:42.508
My feet did not fit in the shoes.

1035
00:40:42.508 --> 00:40:44.000
How do you go from running the Vermont 100

1036
00:40:44.000 --> 00:40:45.870
to running Badwater, I mean the hardest,

1037
00:40:45.870 --> 00:40:48.297
I mean, I paced Dean Karnazes at that race

1038
00:40:48.297 --> 00:40:50.410
and it just, it almost killed me

1039
00:40:50.410 --> 00:40:51.830
just trying to support that guy.

1040
00:40:51.830 --> 00:40:52.663
Yeah, It was hot.

1041
00:40:52.663 --> 00:40:55.870
It was 137 degrees the day we did it, it was hot.

1042
00:40:55.870 --> 00:40:58.840
My shoes melted, my shirt melted. I don't know.

1043
00:40:58.840 --> 00:41:00.920
You know, there's something amazing about the human body.

1044
00:41:00.920 --> 00:41:03.150
The body gets more efficient.

1045
00:41:03.150 --> 00:41:05.526
You would think like, everybody talks to me about,

1046
00:41:05.526 --> 00:41:07.167
well, you gotta recover or whatever.

1047
00:41:07.167 --> 00:41:10.079
And I don't know, if this was the 1700s, 1800s,

1048
00:41:10.079 --> 00:41:13.170
you and I would not like, we wouldn't stop on a mountain

1049
00:41:13.170 --> 00:41:16.430
with our horse and carriage and say it's recovery day today.

1050
00:41:16.430 --> 00:41:17.860
You just keep going.

1051
00:41:17.860 --> 00:41:21.110
And the human body just gets stronger or it dies.

1052
00:41:21.110 --> 00:41:26.046
And so I just found for me, just get stronger.

1053
00:41:26.046 --> 00:41:29.760
Like my fastest time, that 100-mile run in Vermont,

1054
00:41:29.760 --> 00:41:32.210
fly to Vegas, go to Badwater,

1055
00:41:32.210 --> 00:41:35.630
my fastest time in that 235 miles

1056
00:41:35.630 --> 00:41:38.710
was the last 13 miles up to Mount Whitney.

1057
00:41:38.710 --> 00:41:39.753
I was flying.

1058
00:41:40.848 --> 00:41:42.500
That climb is brutal.

1059
00:41:42.500 --> 00:41:44.900
I ran that whole thing, like a lunatic.

1060
00:41:44.900 --> 00:41:46.450
I probably passed eight people.

1061
00:41:47.329 --> 00:41:49.970
And just because your body gets more efficient.

1062
00:41:49.970 --> 00:41:53.363
I'm no special athlete, all I did was BMX as a kid.

1063
00:41:53.363 --> 00:41:55.500
I didn't, what the hell am I? You know.

1064
00:41:55.500 --> 00:41:57.520
The best part is how you finished the marathon

1065
00:41:57.520 --> 00:41:59.620
in Lake Placid though.

1066
00:41:59.620 --> 00:42:01.383
Yeah, yeah, well, what happened was,

1067
00:42:02.726 --> 00:42:04.715
I'm feeling great in the swim.

1068
00:42:04.715 --> 00:42:06.878
We get there, I sit in an ice tub-

1069
00:42:06.878 --> 00:42:08.634
(chuckles) Were you feeling great?

1070
00:42:08.634 --> 00:42:10.400
I'm feeling great in the swim, right?

1071
00:42:10.400 --> 00:42:13.170
Cold water. And I'm crushing it.

1072
00:42:13.170 --> 00:42:15.540
I get on the bike, I'm feeling pretty damn good on the bike.

1073
00:42:15.540 --> 00:42:18.389
And I'm like, you know what, this is gonna be great.

1074
00:42:18.389 --> 00:42:19.810
And within three or four miles on the run,

1075
00:42:19.810 --> 00:42:21.600
I was dead, I was smoked.

1076
00:42:21.600 --> 00:42:24.000
And I asked a couple of kids.

1077
00:42:24.000 --> 00:42:26.853
I said, hey, on the side, do you have any aspirin, advil?

1078
00:42:27.800 --> 00:42:30.426
They said, "No, but we've got beer."

1079
00:42:30.426 --> 00:42:32.110
And I don't drink, give me a beer.

1080
00:42:32.110 --> 00:42:33.640
And I drank one beer, two beers.

1081
00:42:33.640 --> 00:42:36.560
So I basically got drunk, I was eating hamburgers.

1082
00:42:36.560 --> 00:42:40.660
I couldn't consume enough during that marathon.

1083
00:42:40.660 --> 00:42:43.050
Yeah, but you got that done, man.

1084
00:42:43.050 --> 00:42:44.990
That is so impressive.

1085
00:42:44.990 --> 00:42:47.760
And then you've gone on, you did Furnace Creek.

1086
00:42:47.760 --> 00:42:51.360
You did this crazy like 350-mile thing in Quebec

1087
00:42:51.360 --> 00:42:52.310
in the wintertime.

1088
00:42:52.310 --> 00:42:55.260
I biked from Vermont to the Furnace Creek start line.

1089
00:42:55.260 --> 00:42:56.530
Get out.
Yeah.

1090
00:42:56.530 --> 00:42:58.373
That was a 14-day bike ride.

1091
00:42:59.260 --> 00:43:02.180
Oh my God, that was your training.

1092
00:43:02.180 --> 00:43:03.910
That was my training for that. Yeah.

1093
00:43:03.910 --> 00:43:06.410
Yeah. I went nuts. I went nuts.

1094
00:43:06.410 --> 00:43:07.846
Whatever-

1095
00:43:07.846 --> 00:43:11.090
There was a year where you did 50 ultras and 14 Ironmans,

1096
00:43:11.090 --> 00:43:12.340
that's gotta be a record.

1097
00:43:13.185 --> 00:43:14.018
I was in a, what happened,

1098
00:43:14.018 --> 00:43:16.826
the reason that happened is I was in a bad car accident

1099
00:43:16.826 --> 00:43:19.540
and I was thrown out the window at 85 miles an hour.

1100
00:43:19.540 --> 00:43:21.140
My leg was ripped out of my hip.

1101
00:43:22.436 --> 00:43:25.496
So I'm laying in bed, and the doctor was saying,

1102
00:43:25.496 --> 00:43:26.657
you're not gonna be able to walk again.

1103
00:43:26.657 --> 00:43:28.207
And like, crutches, wheelchair.

1104
00:43:29.092 --> 00:43:30.979
And I just kept going to different doctors

1105
00:43:30.979 --> 00:43:32.560
until I got a doctor that said, you'd be fine.

1106
00:43:32.560 --> 00:43:35.740
And Lisa Smith, I don't know if you know her.

1107
00:43:35.740 --> 00:43:38.429
She had gotten me into some of this stuff.

1108
00:43:38.429 --> 00:43:40.963
So she called to say, how are you?

1109
00:43:40.963 --> 00:43:41.920
I heard you got in a car accident.

1110
00:43:41.920 --> 00:43:43.690
I said, look, it doesn't look like I'll be doing

1111
00:43:43.690 --> 00:43:45.210
these Adventure races anymore or whatever.

1112
00:43:45.210 --> 00:43:46.893
She said, why don't we do Ironmans?

1113
00:43:48.259 --> 00:43:51.950
And maybe I had done one Ironman at that point, maybe two.

1114
00:43:51.950 --> 00:43:54.500
And she said, we'll just do every Ironman there is.

1115
00:43:55.682 --> 00:43:57.308
I said all right, let's do it, fuck it.

1116
00:43:57.308 --> 00:43:59.060
'Cause I was just out of the hospital.

1117
00:43:59.060 --> 00:44:01.151
My leg had been ripped out of my hip.

1118
00:44:01.151 --> 00:44:03.617
And a lot of times when you're hurt though,

1119
00:44:03.617 --> 00:44:05.877
you see those athletes come back stronger, right?

1120
00:44:05.877 --> 00:44:07.230
So it just gave me something to look forward to.

1121
00:44:07.230 --> 00:44:09.223
And I just, I went nuts that year.

1122
00:44:10.911 --> 00:44:12.530
It ended, that year ended because my wife

1123
00:44:12.530 --> 00:44:16.480
was eight and a half months pregnant with our first child.

1124
00:44:16.480 --> 00:44:18.396
I was at an Ironman.

1125
00:44:18.396 --> 00:44:22.277
I had just like barely came in, walking on the marathon.

1126
00:44:22.277 --> 00:44:23.440
I was just exhausted, as you can imagine

1127
00:44:23.440 --> 00:44:25.530
from doing all those things.

1128
00:44:25.530 --> 00:44:26.470
And she said, "Are we gonna have a family?

1129
00:44:26.470 --> 00:44:28.560
Or are you just gonna keep doing races?"

1130
00:44:28.560 --> 00:44:29.393
(Rich laughs)

1131
00:44:29.393 --> 00:44:31.330
Yeah, I mean, at some point, you know, it's like,

1132
00:44:31.330 --> 00:44:33.303
what are you running from, you know?

1133
00:44:34.490 --> 00:44:36.800
A lot of this is so nourishing

1134
00:44:36.800 --> 00:44:39.550
because it's connecting you with yourself

1135
00:44:39.550 --> 00:44:43.568
and your potential, and there's so much to be learned about,

1136
00:44:43.568 --> 00:44:46.630
you know, what we're capable of, right?

1137
00:44:46.630 --> 00:44:48.620
But you can also hide behind it.

1138
00:44:48.620 --> 00:44:51.070
Like you can run from your life by just being

1139
00:44:51.070 --> 00:44:52.670
in too many events all the time.

1140
00:44:54.017 --> 00:44:56.530
I had never been married before, so I didn't,

1141
00:44:56.530 --> 00:44:59.850
you know, and I was learning, navigating that.

1142
00:44:59.850 --> 00:45:03.680
But for me, although you might have better insight

1143
00:45:03.680 --> 00:45:05.642
and to me listening to me,

1144
00:45:05.642 --> 00:45:07.599
for me, it was more about like,

1145
00:45:07.599 --> 00:45:09.260
I was so stressed out in business

1146
00:45:09.260 --> 00:45:11.580
that whenever I got to a place where I was like,

1147
00:45:11.580 --> 00:45:15.710
just wanting water, food, and shelter, it was so nice.

1148
00:45:15.710 --> 00:45:17.260
And I just liked getting there,

1149
00:45:18.386 --> 00:45:19.340
just like as long as I fall asleep

1150
00:45:19.340 --> 00:45:21.660
in my soup at dinner, I'm happy.

1151
00:45:21.660 --> 00:45:22.493
Does that make sense?

1152
00:45:22.493 --> 00:45:24.350
Yeah, of course. Like that's what it's all about.

1153
00:45:24.350 --> 00:45:25.880
I mean, what do you think?

1154
00:45:25.880 --> 00:45:29.090
Like, what is that experience for somebody

1155
00:45:29.090 --> 00:45:32.083
who's never been in that position before teach you?

1156
00:45:33.116 --> 00:45:36.730
I guess for me, I needed to meet myself, right?

1157
00:45:36.730 --> 00:45:39.823
I needed to find out who I am. Am I tough enough?

1158
00:45:42.900 --> 00:45:47.320
I liken it to, if I had a Ferrari in the garage,

1159
00:45:47.320 --> 00:45:50.710
and I just looked at it and I polished it and you know,

1160
00:45:50.710 --> 00:45:54.000
showed photos of it, versus I took it out on the track

1161
00:45:54.000 --> 00:45:56.190
and I raced it up against the, you know,

1162
00:45:56.190 --> 00:45:57.863
however you race a race car,

1163
00:45:58.800 --> 00:46:00.790
you get to see how it handles.

1164
00:46:00.790 --> 00:46:02.610
And so I was just, I guess learning to see

1165
00:46:02.610 --> 00:46:06.745
how do I handle, how do I do?

1166
00:46:06.745 --> 00:46:09.090
And you get humble.

1167
00:46:09.090 --> 00:46:11.830
I don't know about you, but I gained integrity

1168
00:46:11.830 --> 00:46:15.301
during all those crazy adventures because,

1169
00:46:15.301 --> 00:46:18.600
I liken that to like a structural engineer

1170
00:46:18.600 --> 00:46:20.720
that checks the integrity of the parts and pieces

1171
00:46:20.720 --> 00:46:23.003
before he puts the building together.

1172
00:46:23.003 --> 00:46:26.860
Like I was testing the integrity of my parts and pieces.

1173
00:46:26.860 --> 00:46:28.870
And I think when you've suffered like that,

1174
00:46:28.870 --> 00:46:30.829
like you've suffered, like I've suffered,

1175
00:46:30.829 --> 00:46:33.560
it knocks the edges off.

1176
00:46:33.560 --> 00:46:36.450
And it's like, you know, when I meet a really tough wrestler

1177
00:46:36.450 --> 00:46:38.610
or a Navy seal or Delta force operator,

1178
00:46:38.610 --> 00:46:40.710
and they've got a somewhat soft handshake,

1179
00:46:42.489 --> 00:46:43.330
they're that way for that reason.

1180
00:46:43.330 --> 00:46:45.180
They've been through some tough shit.

1181
00:46:46.058 --> 00:46:47.860
They don't need to squeeze you, does that make sense?

1182
00:46:47.860 --> 00:46:48.693
Yeah, I get that.

1183
00:46:48.693 --> 00:46:50.110
I mean, I think when I think of you,

1184
00:46:50.110 --> 00:46:51.920
to like extend the car analogy,

1185
00:46:51.920 --> 00:46:54.500
like your RPMs are running so hot all the time,

1186
00:46:54.500 --> 00:46:56.910
because when you get back from that race,

1187
00:46:56.910 --> 00:46:58.330
you're in Wall Street.

1188
00:46:58.330 --> 00:47:01.260
So you're burning it in a different way.

1189
00:47:01.260 --> 00:47:04.783
So there's no like recuperation,

1190
00:47:05.632 --> 00:47:07.520
like it's amazing you didn't just completely flame out.

1191
00:47:07.520 --> 00:47:12.520
Yeah, I had zero recovery, but the other thing-

1192
00:47:12.547 --> 00:47:14.770
(Rich laughs)

1193
00:47:14.770 --> 00:47:17.510
The other thing is, I mean, I remember flying to like,

1194
00:47:17.510 --> 00:47:19.530
you'd be able to look this up if my memory is off,

1195
00:47:19.530 --> 00:47:22.540
but I remember flying once to like South Africa

1196
00:47:22.540 --> 00:47:24.596
to knock out an Ironman,

1197
00:47:24.596 --> 00:47:27.370
literally land on a Friday afternoon,

1198
00:47:27.370 --> 00:47:29.720
put my bike together, wake up in the morning,

1199
00:47:29.720 --> 00:47:30.970
do the Ironman.

1200
00:47:30.970 --> 00:47:34.060
At the finish line, take my bike apart,

1201
00:47:34.060 --> 00:47:36.200
put it in a box and leave.

1202
00:47:36.200 --> 00:47:39.530
And then get back to New York, work however many days I had

1203
00:47:39.530 --> 00:47:41.346
in New York, three days,

1204
00:47:41.346 --> 00:47:43.939
and then fly back to like Western Australia.

1205
00:47:43.939 --> 00:47:45.593
(laughing)

1206
00:47:45.593 --> 00:47:47.910
It's like crazy.

1207
00:47:47.910 --> 00:47:50.070
What are people in the office thinking?

1208
00:47:50.070 --> 00:47:52.798
Well, it cost me. It costs me a lot, right?

1209
00:47:52.798 --> 00:47:54.759
Because then the boss, if the boss-

1210
00:47:54.759 --> 00:47:55.610
You can't be fully present.

1211
00:47:55.610 --> 00:47:57.030
I wasn't present.

1212
00:47:57.030 --> 00:47:58.830
But that's the negative.

1213
00:47:58.830 --> 00:48:00.670
The negative was I was showing my people

1214
00:48:00.670 --> 00:48:02.360
that I wasn't really interested, right?

1215
00:48:02.360 --> 00:48:03.920
So that was bad for business.

1216
00:48:03.920 --> 00:48:07.253
But what was good for business was that became my brand.

1217
00:48:08.240 --> 00:48:09.530
Everybody else's brand on Wall Street

1218
00:48:09.530 --> 00:48:11.093
was like drinking and partying.

1219
00:48:12.057 --> 00:48:15.640
My brand, which didn't exist, you know, at this time

1220
00:48:15.640 --> 00:48:18.173
in the early 2000s, late 90s,

1221
00:48:19.742 --> 00:48:21.839
I'm the adventure guy that takes his customers to do yoga

1222
00:48:21.839 --> 00:48:24.180
and on run, nobody was doing that.

1223
00:48:24.180 --> 00:48:27.283
Yeah, we'd climb stairs for fun. We don't go drinking.

1224
00:48:28.138 --> 00:48:29.354
Yeah, yeah.

1225
00:48:29.354 --> 00:48:31.560
What's so unique about you, and I'm sure looking back,

1226
00:48:31.560 --> 00:48:33.600
you know, hindsight's 20-20,

1227
00:48:33.600 --> 00:48:37.360
but you're this weird amalgam of street hustler

1228
00:48:37.360 --> 00:48:40.814
with this kind of blue collar, you know, aesthetic,

1229
00:48:40.814 --> 00:48:43.490
and also like the Wall Street pedigree.

1230
00:48:43.490 --> 00:48:46.030
But you also have the yoga hippie thing going on

1231
00:48:46.030 --> 00:48:47.510
at the same time.

1232
00:48:47.510 --> 00:48:49.110
And all of these things fuse to create

1233
00:48:49.110 --> 00:48:50.210
this unique individual.

1234
00:48:50.210 --> 00:48:53.250
Like nobody else could be you or do what you do

1235
00:48:53.250 --> 00:48:56.570
without all of those elements like kind of congealing.

1236
00:48:56.570 --> 00:48:58.763
Yeah, and what I think about a lot is,

1237
00:48:59.740 --> 00:49:01.640
'cause we have four children now,

1238
00:49:01.640 --> 00:49:04.460
from 14 down to 8 years old,

1239
00:49:04.460 --> 00:49:06.620
how do you make your...

1240
00:49:06.620 --> 00:49:08.376
There was a great saying in ancient Sparta.

1241
00:49:08.376 --> 00:49:09.480
Like they need to be better than us.

1242
00:49:09.480 --> 00:49:11.100
Our children need to be better than us.

1243
00:49:11.100 --> 00:49:13.810
And so how do you make,

1244
00:49:13.810 --> 00:49:16.080
how do I make my kids better than me?

1245
00:49:16.080 --> 00:49:18.160
And they don't have the yoga crunchy mom

1246
00:49:18.160 --> 00:49:21.190
and the neighborhood and the wise guy next door.

1247
00:49:21.190 --> 00:49:25.940
They have a house where it's so organized for them

1248
00:49:25.940 --> 00:49:28.963
that it's almost like it's a negative, you know?

1249
00:49:30.640 --> 00:49:34.000
That's the problem with, you know, becoming successful.

1250
00:49:34.000 --> 00:49:36.340
Yeah, it's like not good for the kids in some ways.

1251
00:49:36.340 --> 00:49:38.050
Yeah, but you're running this crazy boot camp

1252
00:49:38.050 --> 00:49:41.408
up in Vermont with all these people coming through

1253
00:49:41.408 --> 00:49:42.241
all the time.

1254
00:49:42.241 --> 00:49:43.260
Yeah, so I mean the kids could carry rocks, right?

1255
00:49:43.260 --> 00:49:45.140
The kids could climb ropes.

1256
00:49:45.140 --> 00:49:48.130
But they don't have a wise guy next door that's-

1257
00:49:48.130 --> 00:49:51.130
Right, little street wisdom.

1258
00:49:51.130 --> 00:49:53.460
And so I need to figure that out.

1259
00:49:53.460 --> 00:49:55.389
They're learning Latin.

1260
00:49:55.389 --> 00:49:57.270
(laughing)

1261
00:49:57.270 --> 00:49:58.750
How old are they now?

1262
00:49:58.750 --> 00:50:03.750
I've got a 8-year-old girl, a 12-year-old girl,

1263
00:50:03.920 --> 00:50:06.738
a 13-year-old boy, 14-year-old boy.

1264
00:50:06.738 --> 00:50:11.738
Yeah, I've got two daughters, 16 and 13, two older boys.

1265
00:50:13.450 --> 00:50:15.910
None of them are interested in any of this stuff,

1266
00:50:15.910 --> 00:50:19.260
and I can't get them interested and that's fine.

1267
00:50:19.260 --> 00:50:22.460
I don't need them to be, but I find myself thinking,

1268
00:50:22.460 --> 00:50:24.140
like they'd probably be better off,

1269
00:50:24.140 --> 00:50:26.340
but I don't want to be the guy who's forcing them

1270
00:50:26.340 --> 00:50:28.500
to do something that they really

1271
00:50:28.500 --> 00:50:30.293
just are not wired to do.

1272
00:50:31.128 --> 00:50:33.490
Well, so I forced it and I continue to force it

1273
00:50:33.490 --> 00:50:36.090
every single day, and my theory,

1274
00:50:36.090 --> 00:50:39.080
and we will find out if I completely fuck this up,

1275
00:50:39.080 --> 00:50:41.590
but my theory is they gain,

1276
00:50:41.590 --> 00:50:43.760
they hate it, they hate it, they hate it.

1277
00:50:43.760 --> 00:50:46.980
And then all of a sudden they score a goal at a soccer game

1278
00:50:46.980 --> 00:50:49.770
and they see they're more fit than their peer.

1279
00:50:49.770 --> 00:50:52.590
And then they hear from, "Hey, you're in pretty good shape,"

1280
00:50:52.590 --> 00:50:53.700
the coach say, right?

1281
00:50:53.700 --> 00:50:56.040
And then maybe it starts to stick-

1282
00:50:56.040 --> 00:50:57.430
It clicks.

1283
00:50:57.430 --> 00:50:59.260
And I think I'm seeing that happen,

1284
00:50:59.260 --> 00:51:01.685
but I might be seeing what I want to see.

1285
00:51:01.685 --> 00:51:04.550
But I force it, I force it every day

1286
00:51:04.550 --> 00:51:06.950
and I've been forcing it since my oldest was four years old.

1287
00:51:06.950 --> 00:51:08.585
Yeah. Yeah.

1288
00:51:08.585 --> 00:51:09.910
Well, let's take it to you.

1289
00:51:09.910 --> 00:51:11.400
So basically, at some point,

1290
00:51:11.400 --> 00:51:15.350
you rip the parachute ripcord and you're out of New York

1291
00:51:15.350 --> 00:51:16.860
and you decide to move to Vermont

1292
00:51:16.860 --> 00:51:19.167
and it's gonna be retirement.

1293
00:51:19.167 --> 00:51:21.060
Yeah, so I've got a picture of a red barn

1294
00:51:21.060 --> 00:51:23.140
on the trading desk.

1295
00:51:23.140 --> 00:51:25.800
Probably because in Ithaca, when I was a kid,

1296
00:51:25.800 --> 00:51:27.850
I saw some red barn in a field.

1297
00:51:27.850 --> 00:51:31.160
I mean, Ithaca is pretty idealic, it's pretty nice.

1298
00:51:31.160 --> 00:51:33.330
And that's what I'm visualizing the country to be.

1299
00:51:33.330 --> 00:51:36.850
And I meet my wife and I'm like,

1300
00:51:36.850 --> 00:51:39.550
I want to find a place like that.

1301
00:51:39.550 --> 00:51:42.700
I don't want to be around the craziness I grew up in.

1302
00:51:42.700 --> 00:51:44.530
I want to get away from all these crazy people

1303
00:51:44.530 --> 00:51:48.154
on Wall Street, and we're gonna find a farm.

1304
00:51:48.154 --> 00:51:50.450
And so we find this farm in Vermont,

1305
00:51:50.450 --> 00:51:52.003
Vermont was not in our plan.

1306
00:51:53.199 --> 00:51:56.660
And the mistake I made was I associated,

1307
00:51:56.660 --> 00:51:58.600
and this is like a JV mistake.

1308
00:51:58.600 --> 00:52:03.310
I associated country like Ithaca was country with like,

1309
00:52:03.310 --> 00:52:05.600
but there's academics in Ithaca, right?

1310
00:52:05.600 --> 00:52:07.740
There's a university there, you run into professors,

1311
00:52:07.740 --> 00:52:10.077
like it's a little more developed.

1312
00:52:10.077 --> 00:52:11.670
That like anywhere in the country would be like that,

1313
00:52:11.670 --> 00:52:12.503
but it's not.

1314
00:52:13.819 --> 00:52:17.312
And so we found a farm in Pittsfield, Vermont,

1315
00:52:17.312 --> 00:52:19.677
a tiny little town, 400 residents,

1316
00:52:19.677 --> 00:52:20.570
eight miles from Killington, Vermont,

1317
00:52:20.570 --> 00:52:22.320
the East coast, largest ski resort.

1318
00:52:23.520 --> 00:52:26.140
And you know, there's not a university there.

1319
00:52:26.140 --> 00:52:28.660
So there's a lot of negatives, there are some positives,

1320
00:52:28.660 --> 00:52:30.500
so not a big crap,

1321
00:52:30.500 --> 00:52:32.600
but it's very hard to make a living there.

1322
00:52:33.494 --> 00:52:36.221
And so we bought this farm, my wife was willing to move up.

1323
00:52:36.221 --> 00:52:38.420
We got cows and chickens and goats,

1324
00:52:38.420 --> 00:52:40.200
and I started putting on races.

1325
00:52:40.200 --> 00:52:42.294
I put trails on the side of the mountain.

1326
00:52:42.294 --> 00:52:44.020
I mean we did unbelievable stuff,

1327
00:52:44.020 --> 00:52:45.010
but it's hard to convince people,

1328
00:52:45.010 --> 00:52:46.730
even though it's only a gas tank away from New York,

1329
00:52:46.730 --> 00:52:49.370
it's hard to convince people to come up to Vermont.

1330
00:52:49.370 --> 00:52:52.540
But it ultimately led to me putting on a race

1331
00:52:52.540 --> 00:52:53.600
we call the Death Race.

1332
00:52:53.600 --> 00:52:55.010
Right, right.

1333
00:52:55.010 --> 00:52:58.530
So let's talk about this 'cause this is crazy

1334
00:52:58.530 --> 00:53:00.070
and still exists, right.

1335
00:53:00.070 --> 00:53:01.450
Still exists, and what happened was,

1336
00:53:01.450 --> 00:53:04.524
I was in Lake Placid Ironman one year.

1337
00:53:04.524 --> 00:53:06.530
I don't know when it was, it was pouring rain

1338
00:53:06.530 --> 00:53:07.690
like you read about,

1339
00:53:07.690 --> 00:53:09.123
I mean, crazy rain.

1340
00:53:10.395 --> 00:53:11.760
And for whatever reason,

1341
00:53:11.760 --> 00:53:14.150
I think I had just gotten done with the Eco challenge

1342
00:53:14.150 --> 00:53:16.850
in Fiji where it rained like nonstop.

1343
00:53:16.850 --> 00:53:18.950
I was at some race where it rained nonstop

1344
00:53:18.950 --> 00:53:21.583
for 10 days, nonstop.

1345
00:53:22.650 --> 00:53:24.290
And this is not gonna make sense,

1346
00:53:24.290 --> 00:53:25.670
I'm gonna sound like bullshitting.

1347
00:53:25.670 --> 00:53:27.740
But like I did that Ironman.

1348
00:53:27.740 --> 00:53:29.200
It rained, I didn't even feel,

1349
00:53:29.200 --> 00:53:31.390
I did not even notice it was raining.

1350
00:53:31.390 --> 00:53:33.470
I had just left, you know, 12 days straight

1351
00:53:33.470 --> 00:53:35.540
or whatever it was of rain.

1352
00:53:35.540 --> 00:53:37.140
And I saw a bunch of top athletes,

1353
00:53:37.140 --> 00:53:40.170
men and women quit that race in the middle of it.

1354
00:53:40.170 --> 00:53:43.008
Is that like 2008, or 2009?

1355
00:53:43.008 --> 00:53:44.674
I remember there was one year where it rained like crazy.

1356
00:53:44.674 --> 00:53:46.230
It was craziness. It had to be before that.

1357
00:53:46.230 --> 00:53:48.197
'Cause Death Rate started before that.

1358
00:53:48.197 --> 00:53:49.545
So I said, this is ridiculous.

1359
00:53:49.545 --> 00:53:50.630
The name of the race is Ironman,

1360
00:53:50.630 --> 00:53:53.100
it doesn't say like Ironman, except if it's raining.

1361
00:53:53.100 --> 00:53:54.443
Right. Right.

1362
00:53:55.520 --> 00:53:57.440
And I grew up with a mom who meditated straight

1363
00:53:57.440 --> 00:54:00.390
for 30 days, like this is ridiculous you'd quit

1364
00:54:00.390 --> 00:54:01.990
because of the rain.

1365
00:54:01.990 --> 00:54:04.510
So I'm looking for ways to bring people

1366
00:54:04.510 --> 00:54:07.430
to Pittsfield, Vermont anyway, to our new farm.

1367
00:54:07.430 --> 00:54:09.179
And I'm like, you know what?

1368
00:54:09.179 --> 00:54:10.012
We're gonna have this race called the death race

1369
00:54:10.012 --> 00:54:11.835
and it's gonna emulate life, and it's gonna,

1370
00:54:11.835 --> 00:54:12.668
there's gonna be no rules.

1371
00:54:12.668 --> 00:54:15.530
And it'd be like, if you get out of the swim in this race

1372
00:54:15.530 --> 00:54:18.370
and you get to your bike and the bike seat is missing,

1373
00:54:18.370 --> 00:54:20.938
so what, you gotta do it without a bike seat.

1374
00:54:20.938 --> 00:54:23.060
(Rich laughs)

1375
00:54:23.060 --> 00:54:25.790
That's gonna be what this race is, right?

1376
00:54:25.790 --> 00:54:27.557
'Cause that's what a CEO would do.

1377
00:54:27.557 --> 00:54:28.950
That's what a startup would do.

1378
00:54:28.950 --> 00:54:31.237
But the mindfuck goes way further with this one.

1379
00:54:31.237 --> 00:54:32.660
The mindfuck goes way farther.

1380
00:54:32.660 --> 00:54:36.030
So first year, Doug Lewis,

1381
00:54:36.030 --> 00:54:38.230
Olympics downhill skier shows up.

1382
00:54:38.230 --> 00:54:41.500
The Ashley brothers, two guys from Vermont,

1383
00:54:41.500 --> 00:54:42.810
I don't know any of these guys.

1384
00:54:42.810 --> 00:54:45.630
Two guys from Vermont who eat barbed wire for breakfast,

1385
00:54:45.630 --> 00:54:47.440
grew up on a bad-ass farm,

1386
00:54:47.440 --> 00:54:49.160
where their dad made 'em chop wood for heat,

1387
00:54:49.160 --> 00:54:52.120
their whole life, just tough guys.

1388
00:54:52.120 --> 00:54:56.160
A teacher, a female teacher and a kid I convinced

1389
00:54:56.160 --> 00:54:57.990
from Wall Street to show up.

1390
00:54:57.990 --> 00:55:00.640
It's about eight people, just like the Transcendence Run.

1391
00:55:00.640 --> 00:55:02.820
And they don't know when it's gonna start,

1392
00:55:02.820 --> 00:55:03.890
they don't know what it includes,

1393
00:55:03.890 --> 00:55:08.510
but it's like handsawing through a log, right?

1394
00:55:08.510 --> 00:55:09.860
12 chunks.

1395
00:55:09.860 --> 00:55:11.840
So they're there for like three hours,

1396
00:55:11.840 --> 00:55:14.600
handsawing through this log, splitting that wood,

1397
00:55:14.600 --> 00:55:16.150
crawling through a culvert.

1398
00:55:16.150 --> 00:55:18.310
A culvert is a hole under the ground,

1399
00:55:18.310 --> 00:55:21.123
this was 120 feet long that water rushes through,

1400
00:55:21.960 --> 00:55:24.020
that they could barely fit in,

1401
00:55:24.020 --> 00:55:26.200
putting together a wheelbarrow

1402
00:55:26.200 --> 00:55:29.003
with Japanese directions and no tools.

1403
00:55:30.840 --> 00:55:33.360
But the thing is, you don't tell them when it starts,

1404
00:55:33.360 --> 00:55:35.800
how long it's gonna go for, when it's gonna end,

1405
00:55:35.800 --> 00:55:37.880
or even what they're gonna be doing.

1406
00:55:37.880 --> 00:55:38.713
They had no idea.

1407
00:55:38.713 --> 00:55:40.080
No idea what they were getting into.

1408
00:55:41.085 --> 00:55:43.450
And, the two rugged Vermonters

1409
00:55:43.450 --> 00:55:47.180
and the Olympic downhill skier are battling for first.

1410
00:55:47.180 --> 00:55:50.450
And the teacher is, everybody else quit.

1411
00:55:50.450 --> 00:55:54.077
The female teacher who's in dead last by like five hours,

1412
00:55:54.077 --> 00:55:57.360
and we're trying to get her to quit because I can't manage

1413
00:55:57.360 --> 00:55:58.340
the front of the pack,

1414
00:55:58.340 --> 00:56:00.960
15 miles away from this woman in the back.

1415
00:56:00.960 --> 00:56:03.400
And she says, you'd have to kill me.

1416
00:56:03.400 --> 00:56:06.670
Like she literally just would not quit.

1417
00:56:06.670 --> 00:56:11.670
So ultimately the skier wins and the brothers come in second

1418
00:56:12.300 --> 00:56:16.380
and then the female comes in third after the two brothers.

1419
00:56:16.380 --> 00:56:19.310
And it just, the race just became this thing.

1420
00:56:19.310 --> 00:56:22.850
The New York Times picked up a story and they came

1421
00:56:22.850 --> 00:56:24.270
and filmed it.

1422
00:56:24.270 --> 00:56:25.510
And the world got excited,

1423
00:56:25.510 --> 00:56:27.580
and then all of a sudden 300 competitors every year

1424
00:56:27.580 --> 00:56:29.930
would descend upon Pittsville, Vermont.

1425
00:56:29.930 --> 00:56:34.310
And the race got tougher, and longer and crazier

1426
00:56:34.310 --> 00:56:36.821
with every year.

1427
00:56:36.821 --> 00:56:37.950
So every year you're throwing something

1428
00:56:37.950 --> 00:56:38.830
new at them, right?

1429
00:56:38.830 --> 00:56:40.193
Like it's never the same?

1430
00:56:41.290 --> 00:56:43.370
Didn't you have like a bus where you would stand there

1431
00:56:43.370 --> 00:56:45.988
and say, you can get on the bus and quit for like-

1432
00:56:45.988 --> 00:56:46.828
Oh, you have no idea-

1433
00:56:46.828 --> 00:56:48.940
(laughing) Just toying with them.

1434
00:56:48.940 --> 00:56:50.360
What happened was the two brothers

1435
00:56:50.360 --> 00:56:51.850
who I've since become great friends with,

1436
00:56:51.850 --> 00:56:54.440
the two brothers from that first race,

1437
00:56:54.440 --> 00:56:57.779
they had done lots of big races throughout their life.

1438
00:56:57.779 --> 00:56:59.909
These are tough guys,

1439
00:56:59.909 --> 00:57:01.970
crazy races in the Tundra.

1440
00:57:01.970 --> 00:57:05.180
Like they would have done really well in the 1700s.

1441
00:57:05.180 --> 00:57:08.320
And they took on the persona of the death race

1442
00:57:08.320 --> 00:57:09.480
after doing it that first year.

1443
00:57:09.480 --> 00:57:13.240
They started to make little skull necklaces,

1444
00:57:13.240 --> 00:57:15.750
they got tattoos, like they were just into this.

1445
00:57:15.750 --> 00:57:18.470
And I started to see some of the videos they were creating.

1446
00:57:18.470 --> 00:57:20.120
And they were training specifically

1447
00:57:20.120 --> 00:57:23.390
for what we had just done that first year.

1448
00:57:23.390 --> 00:57:25.740
And I said, they're not gonna do the same stuff

1449
00:57:25.740 --> 00:57:27.830
the next year, I'm gonna change everything.

1450
00:57:27.830 --> 00:57:30.650
And it broke 'em, it drove them crazy

1451
00:57:30.650 --> 00:57:33.489
because they had just trained for eight or nine months,

1452
00:57:33.489 --> 00:57:35.420
whatever it was, to do exactly what they...

1453
00:57:35.420 --> 00:57:37.057
They could put a wheelbarrow together now-

1454
00:57:37.057 --> 00:57:38.927
You're trying to figure out what their weaknesses are

1455
00:57:38.927 --> 00:57:40.377
so you can throw something at them

1456
00:57:40.377 --> 00:57:41.490
that's just gonna derail their whole plan.

1457
00:57:41.490 --> 00:57:44.590
Exactly, and so every year it became,

1458
00:57:44.590 --> 00:57:46.120
what are they expecting?

1459
00:57:46.120 --> 00:57:47.400
What are the competitors expecting

1460
00:57:47.400 --> 00:57:49.861
versus what are we going to throw at 'em?

1461
00:57:49.861 --> 00:57:51.910
(laughs) It's diabolical.

1462
00:57:51.910 --> 00:57:52.910
It was diabolical.

1463
00:57:54.030 --> 00:57:56.240
And so, you know, they would roll into town,

1464
00:57:56.240 --> 00:57:57.410
let's say on a Thursday night,

1465
00:57:57.410 --> 00:58:00.470
getting ready for a Friday afternoon start

1466
00:58:00.470 --> 00:58:02.270
and they'd check into the general store

1467
00:58:02.270 --> 00:58:04.150
and they'd have khakis on and shoes,

1468
00:58:04.150 --> 00:58:05.197
and they'd be with their family, and I'd say,

1469
00:58:05.197 --> 00:58:06.427
"Race starts right now."

1470
00:58:08.480 --> 00:58:09.843
They haven't even signed the waiver yet.

1471
00:58:09.843 --> 00:58:11.730
We're going right now, grab and ax and we're going.

1472
00:58:11.730 --> 00:58:16.410
And so it just became this mystical thing

1473
00:58:16.410 --> 00:58:17.530
that still goes on today.

1474
00:58:17.530 --> 00:58:20.340
Right, and there was a TV deal on the table

1475
00:58:20.340 --> 00:58:22.580
at one point, right, that didn't end up panning out.

1476
00:58:22.580 --> 00:58:24.980
Yeah, we had many, many TV deals.

1477
00:58:24.980 --> 00:58:27.430
We've had so many TV people come up

1478
00:58:27.430 --> 00:58:31.610
from The Discovery Channels, the NBCs and invariably,

1479
00:58:31.610 --> 00:58:34.150
everybody gets really nervous with that word,

1480
00:58:34.150 --> 00:58:35.200
you know, Death Race.

1481
00:58:36.277 --> 00:58:38.360
And it is, it's a crazy race.

1482
00:58:38.360 --> 00:58:40.950
People have come really close to dying.

1483
00:58:40.950 --> 00:58:42.350
And so it-

1484
00:58:42.350 --> 00:58:45.613
How Ironclad is that waiver?

1485
00:58:46.680 --> 00:58:49.630
In the waiver, it says, we will bury you on the property.

1486
00:58:52.470 --> 00:58:54.280
We might not even tell anyone.

1487
00:58:54.280 --> 00:58:56.960
You know, it doesn't get like a lot of media though.

1488
00:58:56.960 --> 00:58:59.042
You know, you don't hear that much about it.

1489
00:58:59.042 --> 00:59:00.910
It did, it did get a ton of media

1490
00:59:00.910 --> 00:59:05.070
in like 2007, 2008, but then from there,

1491
00:59:05.070 --> 00:59:07.830
Spartan was born and Spartan took over

1492
00:59:07.830 --> 00:59:10.540
and then Tough Mudder and Spartan took over.

1493
00:59:10.540 --> 00:59:11.373
Become a thing.

1494
00:59:11.373 --> 00:59:12.700
I mean Spartan is really born out of this idea.

1495
00:59:12.700 --> 00:59:15.530
How do we take what's great about the Death Race

1496
00:59:15.530 --> 00:59:19.490
and make it accessible for the average weekend warrior

1497
00:59:19.490 --> 00:59:21.140
and then scale it up-

1498
00:59:21.140 --> 00:59:23.941
And formalize it, and standardize it.

1499
00:59:23.941 --> 00:59:25.991
Like you're not gonna find wood chopping.

1500
00:59:27.059 --> 00:59:29.670
It was more military based, like standardized for Spartan.

1501
00:59:29.670 --> 00:59:34.670
But Death Race is my baby, and I love torturing people.

1502
00:59:35.090 --> 00:59:37.290
How many people show up these days,

1503
00:59:37.290 --> 00:59:39.382
Still about 300 people.

1504
00:59:39.382 --> 00:59:40.960
And how many people finish?

1505
00:59:40.960 --> 00:59:43.360
Depends on the year, what kind of mood I'm in?

1506
00:59:45.181 --> 00:59:47.110
(laughs) I like how it's up to you.

1507
00:59:47.110 --> 00:59:47.943
It's really up to me,

1508
00:59:47.943 --> 00:59:51.240
because we could go till Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday,

1509
00:59:51.240 --> 00:59:52.909
and we could say, all right,

1510
00:59:52.909 --> 00:59:54.070
we only want three people finishing.

1511
00:59:54.070 --> 00:59:55.900
So imagine you're four days into this,

1512
00:59:55.900 --> 00:59:58.920
just hypothetically, Rich Roll is four days in.

1513
00:59:58.920 --> 01:00:00.770
You can't see straight,

1514
01:00:00.770 --> 01:00:02.660
and there's still 12 competitors.

1515
01:00:02.660 --> 01:00:04.427
And I say to all 12 of you, I say,

1516
01:00:04.427 --> 01:00:07.500
"Guys, I'm here. We'll go as long as you want.

1517
01:00:07.500 --> 01:00:10.490
But until we're down to three, it doesn't end."

1518
01:00:10.490 --> 01:00:12.600
And people start dropping right there.

1519
01:00:12.600 --> 01:00:14.500
It's like Hunger Games.

1520
01:00:14.500 --> 01:00:16.180
Like you're overlooking the whole thing going,

1521
01:00:16.180 --> 01:00:18.860
how can I fuck with these people and improvising-

1522
01:00:18.860 --> 01:00:21.115
It's so easy to break people.

1523
01:00:21.115 --> 01:00:22.170
And what have you learned about the people

1524
01:00:22.170 --> 01:00:24.150
that break last?

1525
01:00:24.150 --> 01:00:27.210
Does it go back to that grit and the mental fortitude

1526
01:00:27.210 --> 01:00:29.620
that is more important than their physical conditioning?

1527
01:00:29.620 --> 01:00:30.950
No doubt about it,

1528
01:00:30.950 --> 01:00:32.030
you could tell right away,

1529
01:00:32.030 --> 01:00:34.940
the big muscular guys, they're out first.

1530
01:00:34.940 --> 01:00:37.340
They're out first and it's a CEO,

1531
01:00:37.340 --> 01:00:40.840
it's a mom that just waits everybody out.

1532
01:00:40.840 --> 01:00:43.250
You must get Navy SEALs and guys like that coming in.

1533
01:00:43.250 --> 01:00:45.836
I lost a Green Beret out there once.

1534
01:00:45.836 --> 01:00:47.260
Literally lost him, we had search dogs, the whole thing.

1535
01:00:47.260 --> 01:00:51.810
We found them in the attic of one of my barns sleeping.

1536
01:00:51.810 --> 01:00:53.500
We had shut the whole race down,

1537
01:00:53.500 --> 01:00:54.840
looking for a Green Beret.

1538
01:00:54.840 --> 01:00:58.073
We've had them all, we've had every form of military,

1539
01:00:59.420 --> 01:01:02.660
MMA, all of them have come out to try to test themselves.

1540
01:01:02.660 --> 01:01:05.210
But you know, a lot of them quit

1541
01:01:05.210 --> 01:01:07.160
in the first three, four hours.

1542
01:01:07.160 --> 01:01:09.703
Wow.
Just like, this is insane.

1543
01:01:09.703 --> 01:01:10.580
This guy's nuts.
Right.

1544
01:01:10.580 --> 01:01:12.320
So Spartan race begins.

1545
01:01:12.320 --> 01:01:14.590
I mean, you're up in Vermont kind of trying

1546
01:01:14.590 --> 01:01:17.900
to be semi-retired and instead you build this empire.

1547
01:01:17.900 --> 01:01:22.535
Yeah, so I'm done, we're having children

1548
01:01:22.535 --> 01:01:25.330
and this was gonna be like my time to recover.

1549
01:01:25.330 --> 01:01:27.070
We talked about recovery.

1550
01:01:27.070 --> 01:01:31.275
And Death Race leads to this idea of Spartan race

1551
01:01:31.275 --> 01:01:35.780
and 700 people show up to that first event in Vermont.

1552
01:01:35.780 --> 01:01:37.610
And I happened to have Discovery Channel.

1553
01:01:37.610 --> 01:01:40.840
There's a guy from Discovery Channel at that first event.

1554
01:01:40.840 --> 01:01:42.410
And he's seeing people across this,

1555
01:01:42.410 --> 01:01:44.609
it's just a three-mile race

1556
01:01:44.609 --> 01:01:45.868
and they're crossing the finish line.

1557
01:01:45.868 --> 01:01:46.701
He's like, "Oh my God, are you seeing

1558
01:01:46.701 --> 01:01:47.650
there's transformations that take place?"

1559
01:01:47.650 --> 01:01:49.350
And I'm not really paying attention to that

1560
01:01:49.350 --> 01:01:50.780
because I've got logistical problems,

1561
01:01:50.780 --> 01:01:51.950
any race organized, you're dealing

1562
01:01:51.950 --> 01:01:53.500
with other stuff, toilet paper.

1563
01:01:54.453 --> 01:01:57.040
And he's like, this is unbelievable what's happening here.

1564
01:01:57.040 --> 01:01:59.310
And that gives me the impetus,

1565
01:01:59.310 --> 01:02:00.660
all right, we're gonna put on a second race

1566
01:02:00.660 --> 01:02:03.208
and a third race and a fourth race.

1567
01:02:03.208 --> 01:02:05.160
And then 1,500 people showed up and then 3000

1568
01:02:05.160 --> 01:02:07.540
and then Tough Mudder came onto the scene.

1569
01:02:07.540 --> 01:02:09.600
And so then I'm a pretty competitive guy,

1570
01:02:09.600 --> 01:02:11.128
then everybody's like-

1571
01:02:11.128 --> 01:02:12.700
That's like not getting into Cornell.

1572
01:02:12.700 --> 01:02:14.840
I gotta bury these guys and be the best.

1573
01:02:14.840 --> 01:02:16.823
I'm gonna win this thing.

1574
01:02:16.823 --> 01:02:19.410
And so if they were in the US and Australia,

1575
01:02:19.410 --> 01:02:21.500
we're going to Slovakia, we're gonna be...

1576
01:02:21.500 --> 01:02:24.381
I'm gonna go anywhere they're not,

1577
01:02:24.381 --> 01:02:26.746
and we're gonna, we're just gonna win this war.

1578
01:02:26.746 --> 01:02:28.920
And really that battle between Tough Mudder and Spartan

1579
01:02:28.920 --> 01:02:31.130
I think drove both of us, the founders,

1580
01:02:31.130 --> 01:02:36.100
me and the founders of Mudder to really build an industry,

1581
01:02:36.100 --> 01:02:38.120
build a sport out of this thing.

1582
01:02:38.120 --> 01:02:40.300
Yeah, the difference from my perspective

1583
01:02:40.300 --> 01:02:44.560
is that with Spartan, there's this elite aspect to it

1584
01:02:44.560 --> 01:02:47.040
where you're able to tap

1585
01:02:47.040 --> 01:02:49.050
into a different caliber of athlete.

1586
01:02:49.050 --> 01:02:53.040
And that allows the weekend warriors to compete alongside

1587
01:02:53.040 --> 01:02:54.890
like these people that are like, you know,

1588
01:02:54.890 --> 01:02:58.160
who become the Emilia Boons and the like,

1589
01:02:58.160 --> 01:03:00.907
who get really celebrated for this stuff.

1590
01:03:00.907 --> 01:03:02.010
Yeah, I mean, that was the difference.

1591
01:03:02.010 --> 01:03:04.610
The difference was they, Tough Mudder went out

1592
01:03:04.610 --> 01:03:07.270
and branded it, it's not a race, right?

1593
01:03:07.270 --> 01:03:09.198
This is an experience.

1594
01:03:09.198 --> 01:03:12.070
And we're actually gonna laugh in the face

1595
01:03:12.070 --> 01:03:13.930
of people wanting a medal at the finish line.

1596
01:03:13.930 --> 01:03:16.378
We're gonna give them an orange headband.

1597
01:03:16.378 --> 01:03:18.247
Like that was their shtick.

1598
01:03:18.247 --> 01:03:19.420
And our thing was, if I'm gonna do this,

1599
01:03:19.420 --> 01:03:20.870
I wanna make it a real sport.

1600
01:03:22.047 --> 01:03:24.320
And honestly, it was a business mistake on my part

1601
01:03:24.320 --> 01:03:26.910
because it was harder to build what I was building,

1602
01:03:26.910 --> 01:03:28.880
They were much more inclusive, like right?

1603
01:03:28.880 --> 01:03:30.340
A bunch of guys high fiving,

1604
01:03:30.340 --> 01:03:32.900
they could skip obstacles and they could just earn

1605
01:03:32.900 --> 01:03:34.300
a headband and drink a bunch of beer.

1606
01:03:34.300 --> 01:03:37.810
I was asking people to like train and work hard.

1607
01:03:37.810 --> 01:03:39.810
And if you didn't do an obstacle, you had to do 30 burpees.

1608
01:03:39.810 --> 01:03:41.310
Who the hell wants to do that?

1609
01:03:42.157 --> 01:03:44.870
So I was losing the battle for a long time,

1610
01:03:44.870 --> 01:03:48.570
but because I'm such, I'm just don't quit,

1611
01:03:48.570 --> 01:03:50.470
we ultimately won.

1612
01:03:50.470 --> 01:03:51.750
Yeah, we ultimately won.

1613
01:03:51.750 --> 01:03:53.300
That is your superpower.

1614
01:03:53.300 --> 01:03:54.860
Just you're the Eveready battery

1615
01:03:54.860 --> 01:03:57.000
that's just never gonna run out of energy.

1616
01:03:57.000 --> 01:03:58.950
My team, my Spartan team says,

1617
01:03:58.950 --> 01:04:01.770
I'm very much like a battery on your phone

1618
01:04:01.770 --> 01:04:06.038
that's like just out of juice, but doesn't die somehow.

1619
01:04:06.038 --> 01:04:09.996
(laughing) Like you're always at 6%.

1620
01:04:09.996 --> 01:04:11.890
Yeah, exactly, and it just doesn't die.

1621
01:04:11.890 --> 01:04:13.850
Aha, that's funny.

1622
01:04:13.850 --> 01:04:16.110
The other thing that's cool about what you do is,

1623
01:04:16.110 --> 01:04:20.512
I mean it would be normal for somebody like you

1624
01:04:20.512 --> 01:04:23.000
who's so focused on building Spartan

1625
01:04:23.000 --> 01:04:24.880
and all the things that you're involved in

1626
01:04:24.880 --> 01:04:26.320
to just be focused on that.

1627
01:04:26.320 --> 01:04:28.690
But you really opened up your farm

1628
01:04:28.690 --> 01:04:30.010
and you have all these people that come through.

1629
01:04:30.010 --> 01:04:31.350
It's like this open door policy,

1630
01:04:31.350 --> 01:04:32.420
like, hey, you want to come here?

1631
01:04:32.420 --> 01:04:35.170
We got a bunk house, stay as long as you like,

1632
01:04:35.170 --> 01:04:37.460
but you're gonna have to do what I tell you to.

1633
01:04:37.460 --> 01:04:39.330
So you're still doing this

1634
01:04:39.330 --> 01:04:42.030
kind of one-on-one coaching and mentoring.

1635
01:04:42.030 --> 01:04:45.110
And there's been some pretty extraordinary success stories

1636
01:04:45.110 --> 01:04:46.450
that have come out of that.

1637
01:04:46.450 --> 01:04:47.793
Yeah, I do it.

1638
01:04:48.827 --> 01:04:49.920
You know, my wife hates it, right,

1639
01:04:49.920 --> 01:04:52.530
because I think I invited three million people

1640
01:04:52.530 --> 01:04:55.700
to the farm once, but people don't really,

1641
01:04:55.700 --> 01:04:57.585
you know, it's a little Tom Sawyer-ish, right?

1642
01:04:57.585 --> 01:04:59.151
It's a little karate kid, right?

1643
01:04:59.151 --> 01:05:01.473
What was his name? Mr. Miyagi, right?

1644
01:05:01.473 --> 01:05:02.520
Like you come here,

1645
01:05:02.520 --> 01:05:04.210
you can paint the fence and do this and that.

1646
01:05:04.210 --> 01:05:06.930
And people don't really want to do it.

1647
01:05:06.930 --> 01:05:08.300
They might not be happy with their life,

1648
01:05:08.300 --> 01:05:09.610
they want to lose weight, whatever it is,

1649
01:05:09.610 --> 01:05:12.088
they're trying to clean up things

1650
01:05:12.088 --> 01:05:13.850
and they think they want to come.

1651
01:05:13.850 --> 01:05:18.060
But in most cases when they get there, they tap out.

1652
01:05:18.060 --> 01:05:21.450
There's a few unbelievable success stories.

1653
01:05:21.450 --> 01:05:22.660
The paples guy, right?

1654
01:05:22.660 --> 01:05:25.220
The apples guy was an amazing success story.

1655
01:05:25.220 --> 01:05:27.923
A truck driver, 300 pounds.

1656
01:05:28.930 --> 01:05:30.840
He probably should have been 160 pounds.

1657
01:05:30.840 --> 01:05:34.100
I got him down to 200 pounds in 30 days.

1658
01:05:34.100 --> 01:05:36.850
He shows up with a little wheelie suitcase.

1659
01:05:36.850 --> 01:05:38.100
He's like, all right, you know,

1660
01:05:38.100 --> 01:05:39.928
where am I staying at or whatever.

1661
01:05:39.928 --> 01:05:40.761
I'm like, up there.

1662
01:05:40.761 --> 01:05:41.594
"Up where?"

1663
01:05:41.594 --> 01:05:43.015
Top of the mountain.
In the rafter.

1664
01:05:43.015 --> 01:05:45.425
(laughs)

1665
01:05:45.425 --> 01:05:46.258
He takes the wheelie suitcase,

1666
01:05:46.258 --> 01:05:47.820
he's literally climbing up the mountain with this,

1667
01:05:47.820 --> 01:05:49.200
like four or five hours.

1668
01:05:49.200 --> 01:05:50.940
Gets up there, I got him in a stone cabin.

1669
01:05:50.940 --> 01:05:52.870
He's like, you know, where's the shower or the kitchen?

1670
01:05:52.870 --> 01:05:54.050
There are none.

1671
01:05:54.050 --> 01:05:57.320
And a big bushel of apples, all you're getting is apples.

1672
01:05:57.320 --> 01:06:01.070
We're hiking every day, lost 100 pounds in 30 days,

1673
01:06:01.070 --> 01:06:04.310
obviously extreme, obviously easy to bounce back from that.

1674
01:06:04.310 --> 01:06:07.770
But like, that's the deal like,

1675
01:06:07.770 --> 01:06:09.690
they can't do it on their own.

1676
01:06:09.690 --> 01:06:12.290
And so I provide that ability to do it-

1677
01:06:12.290 --> 01:06:14.690
Structured environment, and strip everything else away.

1678
01:06:14.690 --> 01:06:18.170
Take the phone away, take the food away.

1679
01:06:18.170 --> 01:06:20.010
And yeah, if they give me their keys

1680
01:06:20.010 --> 01:06:21.510
and they give me their wallet,

1681
01:06:22.601 --> 01:06:23.434
we can get them to succeed at anything

1682
01:06:23.434 --> 01:06:24.580
they want to succeed at.

1683
01:06:25.474 --> 01:06:28.913
But as soon as they have a plan B or a way to escape,

1684
01:06:28.913 --> 01:06:30.970
I can't help 'em.

1685
01:06:30.970 --> 01:06:34.470
Right, so the recidivism rate, you can't control.

1686
01:06:34.470 --> 01:06:35.370
I can't control.

1687
01:06:36.317 --> 01:06:38.460
I mean I had two guys show up two months ago,

1688
01:06:38.460 --> 01:06:41.260
big, jacked guys during COVID.

1689
01:06:41.260 --> 01:06:43.980
I had a bunch of kids come, family friends.

1690
01:06:43.980 --> 01:06:46.180
They sent all the kids, I put them through hell.

1691
01:06:46.180 --> 01:06:48.010
And two guys wanted to come.

1692
01:06:48.010 --> 01:06:49.390
I said, you can come while the kids are here.

1693
01:06:49.390 --> 01:06:51.250
I'm putting on a 14-day, yeah, no problem.

1694
01:06:51.250 --> 01:06:53.920
And they spoke with like military lingo.

1695
01:06:53.920 --> 01:06:55.330
They were not military guys.

1696
01:06:55.330 --> 01:06:58.680
And they were gonna crush it. Three hours.

1697
01:06:58.680 --> 01:07:02.323
Kids stayed 14 days. These guys made it three hours.

1698
01:07:02.323 --> 01:07:05.190
Wow. So what's the differentiator there?

1699
01:07:05.190 --> 01:07:07.673
They could leave. They could leave.

1700
01:07:08.514 --> 01:07:11.020
I mean, when you can leave, you take that option.

1701
01:07:11.020 --> 01:07:13.640
And I learned that, you know, in this town,

1702
01:07:13.640 --> 01:07:16.160
we didn't talk about, I rebuilt a general store

1703
01:07:16.160 --> 01:07:17.400
that had been around from the 1800s.

1704
01:07:17.400 --> 01:07:20.310
I rebuilt a farm that have been around from the 1800s.

1705
01:07:20.310 --> 01:07:22.470
And I envisioned finding some entrepreneurs,

1706
01:07:22.470 --> 01:07:24.660
like I was back in the neighborhood

1707
01:07:24.660 --> 01:07:26.667
to come run these things.

1708
01:07:26.667 --> 01:07:28.900
It'd be cool to come run a general store, right?

1709
01:07:28.900 --> 01:07:31.110
And you're not gonna have to get a mortgage and all that.

1710
01:07:31.110 --> 01:07:32.650
It's all paid for. It's all ready for you.

1711
01:07:32.650 --> 01:07:34.303
Turnkey, just walk in.

1712
01:07:35.237 --> 01:07:36.520
And this farm has a tractor and cows,

1713
01:07:36.520 --> 01:07:38.008
all you gotta do is work.

1714
01:07:38.008 --> 01:07:39.021
You just gotta do the work.

1715
01:07:39.021 --> 01:07:41.010
You're going to crush it because you don't have the strains

1716
01:07:41.010 --> 01:07:43.610
that any other entrepreneur has, where you got to borrow,

1717
01:07:43.610 --> 01:07:46.050
and you got to figure out ways to make this work.

1718
01:07:46.050 --> 01:07:48.260
I even have a place for you to live.

1719
01:07:48.260 --> 01:07:51.520
They all quit, and they quit because-

1720
01:07:51.520 --> 01:07:53.100
They're not invested personally.

1721
01:07:53.100 --> 01:07:54.300
They're not invested.

1722
01:07:54.300 --> 01:07:57.690
Like when you write a check and you sell your kids

1723
01:07:57.690 --> 01:07:59.657
and do whatever you have to do to make it,

1724
01:07:59.657 --> 01:08:00.490
you have no choice.

1725
01:08:00.490 --> 01:08:03.789
And so that's the big difference.

1726
01:08:03.789 --> 01:08:06.220
You know, with the Death Race, they were all quitting.

1727
01:08:06.220 --> 01:08:08.260
And then one year I said, here's what we're going to do.

1728
01:08:08.260 --> 01:08:09.460
If you want to do the Death Race,

1729
01:08:09.460 --> 01:08:12.050
you have to get an article written in a local newspaper

1730
01:08:12.050 --> 01:08:15.140
that says you are doing this race and you're gonna finish.

1731
01:08:15.140 --> 01:08:19.173
And all of a sudden finishing rates went up, it's that easy.

1732
01:08:20.170 --> 01:08:21.860
Right?
That's amazing.

1733
01:08:21.860 --> 01:08:25.590
All these people that have come through the farm,

1734
01:08:25.590 --> 01:08:27.540
all the people that you've seen compete

1735
01:08:27.540 --> 01:08:31.225
in these Spartan races, all the athletes that you've,

1736
01:08:31.225 --> 01:08:32.180
you know, run shoulder to shoulder with

1737
01:08:32.180 --> 01:08:33.883
over the course of these races,

1738
01:08:34.761 --> 01:08:37.120
what are the lessons that you take away

1739
01:08:37.120 --> 01:08:40.100
from all of these experiences that can inform

1740
01:08:40.100 --> 01:08:42.720
the person who's at the starting gate

1741
01:08:42.720 --> 01:08:44.520
of a journey like this?

1742
01:08:44.520 --> 01:08:46.600
Well, so I'm a big subscriber in this idea

1743
01:08:46.600 --> 01:08:48.670
of fire, ready, aim, right?

1744
01:08:48.670 --> 01:08:50.730
Just get started. Just take that first step.

1745
01:08:50.730 --> 01:08:52.313
Just go outside. You know this,

1746
01:08:53.629 --> 01:08:55.190
I think we all get stuck sitting around.

1747
01:08:55.190 --> 01:08:58.470
Our brains are wired to avoid discomfort.

1748
01:08:58.470 --> 01:09:00.730
And so I find myself, I know every day,

1749
01:09:00.730 --> 01:09:02.080
I gotta take my cold shower

1750
01:09:02.963 --> 01:09:05.250
and I find myself looking at my phone and I'm like,

1751
01:09:05.250 --> 01:09:07.080
why the fuck am I looking at my...

1752
01:09:07.080 --> 01:09:07.913
Oh, I'm looking at my phone

1753
01:09:07.913 --> 01:09:08.760
because I don't want to get in the cold shower.

1754
01:09:08.760 --> 01:09:11.590
Subconsciously you know, my brain is telling me

1755
01:09:11.590 --> 01:09:14.460
to avoid that pain I'm about to go deal with.

1756
01:09:14.460 --> 01:09:16.800
So just take that first step,

1757
01:09:16.800 --> 01:09:18.420
and that first step leads to another step

1758
01:09:18.420 --> 01:09:20.950
and before you know it, you get it done.

1759
01:09:20.950 --> 01:09:22.430
And that might be signing up for something,

1760
01:09:22.430 --> 01:09:23.420
that might be telling a friend,

1761
01:09:23.420 --> 01:09:24.700
you're gonna go do something,

1762
01:09:24.700 --> 01:09:26.590
or maybe just having a friend ring your bell in the morning

1763
01:09:26.590 --> 01:09:29.890
or having your shoes next to your bed.

1764
01:09:29.890 --> 01:09:32.890
Whatever that is, just take that first step.

1765
01:09:32.890 --> 01:09:36.502
Number two, and I'm doing this a long time.

1766
01:09:36.502 --> 01:09:39.960
So I think you have to have a frame of reference

1767
01:09:39.960 --> 01:09:42.860
that you could fall back on when the going gets tough.

1768
01:09:42.860 --> 01:09:44.270
So what do I mean by that?

1769
01:09:44.270 --> 01:09:48.090
Like, I always go to a place when the, and I say, well,

1770
01:09:48.090 --> 01:09:49.931
it could be worse.

1771
